      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>ASR YOUR GROCI,;R Ft It MC )( )ÜbrAl.i pATT.,NT SEL F- ISDIU Ti U</head>
        <p>ASR YOUR GROCI,;R Ft It MC )( )ÜbrAl.i pATT.,NT SEL F- ISDIU Ti U</p>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>, The votaries of fashion are permitted to evinee theiream individuality in the cut and style of their garments, for to be original while keeping within the bounds of fashion is viewed as the height of perfection in the world of dress. We may strike the picturesque note in the graceful flowing empire modes, or affect a leaning towards the fashions of the early Victorian era, as best suits our fancy, each and all, if exploited with care will achieve success. The first named modes are best suited to Hob and costly materials, such as velvet, satin or brocade, as more opportunity is given to display the beauties of the material; while more suited to =sling, silks, and diaphanous fabrics an</p>
        <p>, I . '*ffp. ' • ' , \ , '‘, ' . i, i . , , , ‘ A ~.. - ; N\ • I,), t t ./.*,..L,1 A 4160_--:- /i ii ' •:..y..W , t, , . ,-,''c I i ....________--------4.,:.‘ , • ,ot.; , .:14,___ .404k5..,.. „t -- - 1 rilAttleCtiriqo i •-.- .:4.;••'J-Wr . A . ;4101 -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>PRETTY ETENING WI.?.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY ETENING WI.?.</p>
        <p>the much drawn or liberally flounced and frilled skirts of the 1830 period, the bodice completed by a lace berthe and quaint tucker. Opera and theatre cloaks and wraps naturally meet with much attention now that the winter iieaeon has fairly set in. A cosy and pretty evening wrap has its charms set forth in my first illustration. It is made of pale eau de nil cloth, lined with coral pink silk, and has a deep cape-like collar and border of soft white fur.</p>
        <p>The shape is a simple one and is yet becoming and fashionable. It would look very well made of a bright emerald green cloth, or velvet trimmed with black fur and lined with white quilted silk._</p>
        <p>Another fashionable and comfortable shape is the kimono, for with its wide sleeves and loose fronts it has proved its value as one of the most admirable of this season's innovations. Of course, there are many much more elaborate cloaks to be seen, some of which are flounced from hem to neck with lace, or covered with rows of chenille fringe; while deep capes, or pelerines of lace and fur, ending in long stole-like ends, cover the shoulders.</p>
        <p>Such beautiful " confections " are only for the few, as their costliness places them out of rease al , most of us.</p>
        <p>VI e. 1 .1 fAI 4) t i • / I ' .. • t ' • 417,11011114 V : iegyAriallkm.iiii*,,,, *i". 10 446 AO 114 07.16 • , *"*AlilliliVklll 1 ".1114</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>LATEST SKIRT.</head>
        <p>THE LATEST SKIRT.</p>
        <p>Green is a very fashionable colour at presoak and, when a good shade is selected, looks as well in the evening as day. It is to be seen in all shades, ranging from the delicate tones of can de nil to the rich deep shades of the emerald.</p>
        <p>The skirt sketched is cut on the latest lines, and is made of dark green clozh and trimmed with chenille fringe ; while a band of silk open embroidery extends down the front. It is made with a series of shaped flounces, which produce the effect very much of one skirt over another.</p>
        <p>Double skirts and tuniqnes are much in evidence for evening wear; while others again have a deep volant headed with lace. Indeed, all the skirts for evening wear this season are rather elaborate and show a variety of trim- ming!, narrow ribbon being much used; while cut jet and sparkling sequins are liberally em- ployed to produce the glistening effect tai,hionab!e.</p>
        <p>Lace also makes one of the prettiest trim- mings. The possessors of squares or shawls of old lace which, till lately, they have viewed in the light of valuable but useless possessions, will find many and various uses for them, as the present modes offer liberal scope for tke disposal of them, so as to form the very prettiest parts of the most elegant toilettes. They can 1):? used to drape across the shonldeis of gowns or evening wraps, the long ends falling on either side of the fronts.</p>
        <p>1, .. . ', r t ~ . I a Ok A ~;,,,f;;;•ifyi jk, . ...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>SMART COAT AND HAT.</head>
        <p>A SMART COAT AND HAT.</p>
        <p>Indeed, these scarves or shawls, draped care- lessly round the shoulders, are quite the most fashionable adjuncts of dress, and are worn with ordinary as well as Empire dress. The cold winters which are generally our fate cause us to appreciate thoroughly a fur-trimmed coat and hat such as is shown in my third illustration.</p>
        <p>It is made of cloth to match the skirt— though velvet could be substituted if preferred —and is cut in double-breasted fashion with a large collar and revers of ermine. There is a smart little basque which forms part and parcel of the shaped waist band. The ermine toque is very smart, and is trimmed with an ostrich plume and an up-standing aigrette. The muff is en suite.</p>
        <p>-One Of the latest n—o;:eilie's in the millinery world are the roses and other flowers made of silk ribbon. These are used very much for trimming the small felt turban and marquise hats, and are a pleasant change from the feather pom-poms one so continually sees. Pinked out mailings of shaded thin silk are employed to form the brims of felt or velvet toques and are becoming, having a softening effect on the face.</p>
        <p>Ostrich feathers are as popular as ever, probably by reason of their becomingness, but are arranged on the hat in a rather different fashion from formerly. They are now laid flatly on the crown of a hat or toque, the stems pointing towards the face, while the tips curl gracefully on the hair behind.</p>
        <p>COQUETTE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The King and Queen fully intend to pay State visits to Scotland and Ireland, next Spring. Only his Majesty's illness necessitated a postponement of these plans, and we have now the authoritative statements of Sir Daniel</p>
        <p>Dixon, the Lord Mayor of Belfast, and of Dr. William- son, recently the guest of the King at Balmoral, that they will be carried out early next year. In all proba- bility their Majesties will be the guests of the Marquis of Londonderry at Mountstewart, near Belfast. and a visit may also be paid to Lisnaore Castle, the Duke of Devonshire's seat an Ireland. At Edinburgh, the King and Queen will stay at Holyrood Palace, which has been redecorated and put in order for their Majesties' use.— According to Dr. Williamson, it is fully intended to revive the Royal memories connected with Holyrood Palace, and it as not unlikely that the King will time his visit so as to himself open the General Assembly of the Church of Scotland next May. At Mountstewart, the Marquis of Londonderry's seat, there is a model of the celebrated Temple of the Winds at Athens, and every visitor to Edinburgh knows the unfinished monument at Calton Hill, which was intended to be a model of the Parthenon at Athens. The King,it is understood, has some idea of initiating a movement for the •completion of the monument, in memory of those who have fallen in the South African War. The original intention was to commemorate the slain at Waterloo, and the foundation stone was laid by the Duke of Hamilton in the year 1822 when George IV. visited Edinburgh. But only sufficient money was raised to erect thirteen columns, and it hall ever since remained an unfinished work for want of funds. All these years it has been a monument to the lack of public spirit, but there is no doubli that with Royal support, amplemeans would be forthcoming to complete the original plans. Frequently of late, have our Royal Princesses been seen out 'on Christmas shopping intent, but the King and Queen make their purchases at home. For this purpose their Majesties very considerately eye their time at Buckingham Palace, as it is so much easier for the shopkeepers to send their wares there, than to Sandringham or Windsor. A large quantity of goods have to be sent, on account of the numerous presents, which the King and Queen, and various members of the Royal Family give away at Christmas. Nor are these confined only to valuables and keepsakes, as their Majesties continue the custom of our late Queen. in sending plum puddings, mince pies, and other items of Christmas fare, to near relatives both at home and abroad. Most of our Christmas observances come from the Continental nations, and it is probablyin Germany that Santa Claus and other traditions may be seen at their best. But a very interesting custom as the Children's Fair, which is held at Stockholm, on Christmas Eve, when the townspeople' including the King and Queen, go out into the market place to buy toys for all the poor children in the town. Such a custom would scarcely be possible in London, or in our great cities, but at the same time none need fail to enter into its spirit for want of opportunities. There are so many Xmas funds and little charities for giving treats and gifts to poor children, at Christmas time, that all can help in this good work if they want to. Few would wish to interfere with the waits at the proper season, but a good many people, in some parts of London and several provincial towns, have had to complain of a premature appearance of-these musicians, who are evidently stimulated very much more by the hope of gain than by a desire to celebrate Xmas. The music of a well trained band on Christmas Eve, or thereabouts is welcome to many people, but that is quite a different thing from being disturbed a fortnight before, by a band winch is more noisy than musical. ' From time to time wealthy people have left money to a regular fund in Paris, to be distributed amongst well-conducted young women, and quite recently a lady handed over 10,000 francs to the municipal authorities, on condition that this sum was to be given to the best be- haved unmarried milliner. Naturally the prospect of being able to go into business on her own account put all the milliners on their best behaviour, alarge number of whom entered their names on the books as eligible for the prize. But apart from any inducements, the Par a workgirl is, as a rule, one of the best behaved in the world. She does not earn high wages, but she is invariably neatly dressed, and manages by frugal economy to keep herself in fairly comfortable circumstances.</p>
        <p>Dainty evening sacques, or "coffee coats," are much in request by the fashionable woman when dining quietly at home. These are built of the lightest fabrics, over soft silk slips. Lace may perhaps be said to rank first in favour, but accordion-pleated chiffon makes up quite as well. The sleeves, which are usually to the elbow only, are invariably open,and in the chiffon coats show ruffles of lace, the loose collar being of lace to match. No garment could be more easy to build, and the home dressmaker cannot fail, with the aid of a good pattern to turn it out a success. Remnant from the Summer rales should sot be overlooked with such a "confection" in view.</p>
        <p>Fur cravats are an innovation, though perhaps as the doctors will probably say, a not very wise one. The long band, about an inch and a half in width in the centre, and widening out at the ends to about three inches, is wound round the throat, and the ends are brought to the front where they hang straight down on either side, stole fashion. Speaking of fur, the disappearance of the beautiful and becoming chinchilla, is distinctly puzzling Another neglected fir is astrachan, carscul having, for. the time being, quite taken its place in our affections. Truly Dame Fashion is quite as capricious in the way of furs as in everything else. With the arrival of the really cold 'weather the turban built of fur has been very popular, and when the brim and the hair match, or nearly so, the effect is often a most undesirable one. Possessors of dark hair should be careful when choosing a turban of sable or other dark fur, to introduce between the hair and brim a line of relief. Some milliners use white fur for this purpose, others employ chiffon of some light, bright shade. Light coloured or white furs and pale coloured tresses should also be divided by a line of relief, the colour employed of course finding a corresponding note in the toilette. "The Noisy Years," by Mabel Dearmer )Smith,Elder, and Co.), is a delightful study of two children, Robin and Toby, which both little folks and their elders cannot fail to enjoy. With the sorrows of Toby, one and all will sympathise, but it is taken altogether, a cheery book, and many of the chapters are amusing in the I extreme. _ _</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>POISONING MYSTERY. Exhumation of the First Mrs. Chapman.</head>
        <p>THE POISONING MYSTERY. Exhumation of the First Mrs. Chapman.</p>
        <p>t,lu</p>
        <p>niM</p>
        <p>" The b.c,-ai of Mrs. Chapman, the first wife of George Chapman, the Southwark publican, who is accused of the wilful murder by the administration of arsenic of Maud Marsh, a barmaid in his employ, on Tuesday was exhumed at St. Patrick's Cemetery, Leytonstone, Lon- don. Mrs. Chapman died on Christmas Day, 1897, at a public house just off the City-road, of which Chapman was landlord before he became tenant of the Monu- ment, in Union-street. Chapman's second wife was buried at Lymm Church- yard. Her body was exhumed two weeks ago under an Home ()Mee order, and in the presence of Dr. Steven- son, the Government pathological expert, who made a Ipost-mortem examination of the remains. The exhumation on Tuesday was conducted with Ilsecrecy. Not even the issue of the necessary Home Office order had been allowed to be made public. The body lay in the north-west corner of the cemetery in what is known as a common grave, i.e., a grave for which fees had been paid but which had not been exclusively retained by the family of the deceased. The work of disinterment began at 10.90 on Monday night. The work proceeded steadily until five o'clock on Tuesday morning, by which time six coffins had been raised. After a brief interval operations' were resumed, and were not concluded until eight a.m., when the remains of Mrs Chapman were reached. The coffin was at once carried to the tool shed erected in a corner of the churchyard, and there remained until the arrival at 10.30 of Dr. Stevenson and his assistant, who made the post- mortem examination, after which the body was re- interred.</p>
        <p>WINTER REMEDIEs.—Non. that the winter is with us it may not be amiss to call attention to the reasonable and effective remedies for simple ailments afforded by the famous "Linseed" specialities. Perhaps the most popular of these lathe well-known "Linseed Compound" (Kay's Compound Essence of Linseed), a standard remedy for coughs, colds, hoarseness, asthma, bronchi- tis, and similar ailments. This has now been to the front for over thirty-five years. Another speciality deserving of notice is "Kay's Tic Pills," one of the few things that will give relief in CRSEIS of tic or neuralgia. "Linum Catharticum Pills," another name familiar to us, is prepared from the extract of the Linum Catharticum, the mountain flax of our English commons, a true natural aperient. With a few good home remedies like the above on hand most of the simple ailments common to the season can be prevented.</p>
      </div>
