      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>EXAIVIINER FRID 'Y DECEMBER 12 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAIVIINER FRID 'Y DECEMBER 12 1902</p>
        <p>OS suAt •ad.itsuu pusoo I irs sua s I !Ilya 0,,</p>
        <p>cc 'We 9291 P stlll 5Ut U[.osie .?utz!sap •eni ume.tp ut aback. This F.. et-owl snock shook me almost MOM than the coming of the money. "Atid why not?" he asked, stopping in the gate- way of the park and awaking almost passionately. "Why not, Rachel? Give me a Bond-street suit of clothes instead of a footman's coat, and, tell me, should I have no chance? I am well read,, better read than many of the young fellows that buzz around her. I have my father's hands that von so clten tease him about, and, abova all, I have an upright soul, and a clean life to offer her. Yon know it, Rachel." "It is true, Paul t but—" . ,</p>
        <p>The stable-clock chimed ten as was speaking, and with a hasty "good-night" Paul raced away across the park. He would be late for prayers 'bras certain, and not see Miss Irma that night very likely. Miss Irma! I Was fairly bewildered. It was preposterous. And yet, as Paul asked, why?</p>
        <p>She knelt down beside me, and laid her two soft, white, tmglored bands ir my lao. "I always admired you very much, Rachel,". she said solemnly—" I can't t.,11 you how much." And then we both lauoed, such spontaneous laughter that even Lady Isabella joined in it briefly. "Really, Irma, have you no shame ?" said my lady.</p>
        <p>"Why should I?" said Irma. "I was taught, before I could walk, to put my arms round cookie's nee:: and ki her for a calcie."</p>
        <p>"But Rachel is not offering you a calsie," said Lady Isabella, a littla grimly.</p>
        <p>"But she will," said Irma. "I scent it in the air," and she put her arm about my neck and rubbed her sweet, soft cheek against mine. "Rachel used to give me a sweetie on the sly when papa's sermon was extra long." "Cupboard love," exclaimed a voice that sounded just a trifle jealous, and Mr. Gerald came in, adding, as he crossed to greet his mother: "Don't trust her, Rachel."</p>
        <p>"Ho is jealous, Rachel," said Irma, in an audible whisper. "He is brimming over with cup- board love himself, but he daren't put his arm round your neck or sit on your lap."</p>
        <p>And she suited the action to the wore, and plumped her slim form on to my knitting, looking across at Gerald as she did so with a saucy, defiant air that was infinitely charming. _</p>
        <p>I could not wonder that Paul had learnt. to worship her. I only marvelled that I had not fore- seen the possibllity. It was not only that she was exceptionally pretty in face and form, but she was so whimsically charming in all her ways, so erratic and unexpectel, so full of an exuberant vitality, with which she combined a unique drawl in speak- ing that was entirely natural and unaffected. She would play with a baby, or frisk with a kitten, quite as eagerly and vlva.ciously as she Would dance at a ball, or ride after the hounds. She held us all in her spell. Paul would have served her (log-like all his life, but for the coming of the money. It was in the power of money to lift him to Irma's level, if he could blot out the past. Could he blot out the past ? A part of Irma's fascination lurked, I thought, in the fact that she was a thorough little aristocrat. Could money make her forget that Paul had been her footman ? It is true that he would never have been her footman could be have seen any aher way of being near her and serving her. Paul was intrinsically worth of a better postiion. 'Would she recognise that? It was a difficult problem for my unaccustomed brain; but I realised to the full that the life's happiness of my favourite brother lay in the little hands that were clasped round my neck. Would she crush it remorselessly, or would she give it in full abundance? It would be one or the other. Irma dim- not favour half-measures, but, well as I loved her, long as I had known her, I could not say which she would do.</p>
        <p>As these thoughts passed through my mind, and Irma sat lightly in my lap—very much impeding my work—Mr. Gerald was hurrying about rather fussily, and presently came over to a little table near us with his arms full of jewel- cases. These he laid upon the table, as be looked hal f -mischievously, half-beseechingly, towards Irma.</p>
        <p>She jumped up, as he had -hoped she would, and went over to him. There was never any pretence, any make-believe, about Irma Bathurst. All her sins were open sins. Her faults of vanity and covetrmsness, and even greediness, were so frankly ex .ressed as t.o disarm reproof, or even d ,pproval. One could only be amused as one is with a pretty child who says frankly "Ale 'ike oo."</p>
        <p>Mr. Gerald had persuaded his mother into letting him teaze Miss Irma with a sight of her diamonds. My lady did not often like them taken out, for they were valuable brilliants, and it seemed just as well not to let everyone know that they were kept in tbe bureau in her studio with- out any patent lock. They had been given to her by her father before her marriage, when she was one of the belles of society, and she had but rarely worn them since. She used to say they were unsuited to the wife of a clergyman, even though he were also a rich squire. It made me smile to see Miss Irma's delight over the flashing stones. To sue they were flm.hing stones and nothing more; but Mies Irma went down on her knee.; beside the table, and pressed her dainty cheek lovingly upon the baubles, as she had pressed it awhile before against me. Mr. Gerald watched her with all his heart in his eyes. The pretty fluttering action of her dimpled hands, the smiles around her mouth, the quick colour ebbing and flowing in her cheeks, and then the great blue eyes lifted to his face, and her fascinating drawl: "Aren't they lovely, Gerald?" I could read in his face the burning desire he had to toss them all at her feet, and beg her only to wear them.</p>
        <p>I daresay lrma read the same, but she took all homage as a matter of course, as hers by right. It did not excite her.</p>
        <p>He lifted a sparkling but somewhat ponderous tiara from its case and said:</p>
        <p>"If this were re-set there would be enough stones for a brooch and earrings, as well as for a tiara of lighter make"; and he held the glittering thing critically at arm's length. "If it were mine," said Irma, "I should have a necklace made of those larger stones, an a butterfly for the hair of the smaller ones. Tiaras are out of fashion, and earrings are always barbarous."</p>
        <p>"So they are," said Gerald. "Mother, why don't you have this old-fashioned tiara re-set and altered? "</p>
        <p>Lady Isabella glanced languidiy at the jewels. "I have thought of it, she said. "I should like a lighter tiara and a brooch. I might wear a brooch myself sometimes, but it would cost a good deal, I think." "Let me take it to town to-morrow, mother," said Gerald eagerly. "I am going up to my tailor, and I could ask Clough and Hughes about it." "I don't mind your asking, Gerald, if you will take care not to lose the tiara; and be sure and let me know the cost before instructing them to do anything." _ _</p>
        <p>"Perhaps I shall get myself a diamond phi when I am there," said Gerald, still gazing critically at the tiara, "diamonds are very effective." . .</p>
        <p>"Men are so selfish," said Irma.. "You had better get diamond waistcoat buttons while you are about it."</p>
        <p>"Perhaps I shall get something for someone else. A butterfly for the hair, for instance," said Gerald insinuatingly. "You vin't afford to," said Irma frankly. "Mr. Densmore says if his horse wins its next race he will give me a gild arrow with a diamond tip as a thank offering. Re has named his horse 'Miss Irma.'"</p>
        <p>Gerald flushed angrily, but be chewed no other signs of annoyance, and only answered: "If I win my wager I will give you the butterfly." "What wager ? " asked Irma, with quick interest. "Oh, there is the luncheon bell. lam ravenous. Ido hope there is pheasant. for lunch, Gerald, and apple fritters. Mrs. Pike makes such heavenly apple fritters." "Girls are so selfish," said Gerald.</p>
        <p>"I am not selfish, Gerald. I don't want all the pheasant and fritters, only a fair share." "Well, you're greedy anywa,y," and their wrangling voices died away down the long corridor. They had known each other from childhood, and Gerald had always been Irma's devoted admirer. But she was not yet eighteen, and her father dis- approved of long engagements, or very early marriages. He gave Mr. Gerald to understand plainly that there was to be no definite love-making at present. Mr. Gerald did not mind this so long as he had the field pretty well to himself, and other sweethearts were hut birds of passag,.. But lately Mr. Densmore had (ome upon the scene. He was the junior partner in an enormously wealthy brewing concern, was in Parliament, kept a few race-horses, had built himself a very grand house a few miles from the Chase, and made no ,secret of his admiration for Miss Irma. She, on her side, made no difficulty about accepting any of the pretty things he offered her, if they chanced to be things she liked, or wanted; but beyond that she shelved him no favour at all, and evidently considered that the obligation was on his side rather than upon hers. She had a sweet, queenly way of accepting favours as she accepted admira- tion, as her natural right, and we all loved to offer them to her; rich and poor, masters and, servants, we all did our best to sp,oil her. No one could help it. .</p>
        <p>And Paul spoilt her more than anyono else. What, I wondered, Was to be Paul's next move? The coming of money had filled my usually placid mind with troubled questioning that I could not set aside. I was so fond of Paul, and so justly proud of him, I longed to learn what he intended to do. Something unusual I WWI quite sun.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>WEDDING Ramos, GENE RINGS.</p>
        <p>FOP, VaLIIF, VISIT</p>
        <p>CHARLES WOLF,</p>
        <p>WHOLESALE JEWELLER, Only Address :</p>
        <p>o, RENSHAW ST., LIVERPOOL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>IIIIAA'S lITOOTNTI</head>
        <p>IIIIAA'S lITOOTNTI</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>By ELLEN TIGHE HOPKINS,</p>
        <p>Author of "When _ Her ? "</p>
        <p>"A Lost Lover," "Jack and .n1l," I was Twenty," "Will Ile Win "Reality," An.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CHAPTER L</head>
        <p>CHAPTER L</p>
        <p>I was twenty-seven when the great news came that made me a rich woman. I remember the day well. A wild, gusty day in October, with low- lying masses of dark cloud, and brightly-tinted autumn leaves, whirling over the great park that surrounded Dudley Chase. My mistress—the Lady Isabella Dudley—had suffered more acutely than usual that day, and I was not sorry when she had. me give up my reading aloud and shut out the twilight. Her studio, for painting was her chief solace, was on the first floor, and I paused moment before drawing thick curtains across the wide window, to watch the swift, silvery rain driving across the hills that rise above the rich vale of Aylesbury. All phases of Nature to me are lovely, but I have a peculiar delight in watching clouds, and the rain that falls from them, drift rapidly across a wide stretch of hill and dale. And Dudley Chase, the home of the Rev. Sir George and the Lady Isabella Dudley, was set high amongst the hills and vales of Aylesbury, and my lady would spend hours on her sofa in that window painting whilst I read to her.</p>
        <p>When. I had closed the curtains and lighted the softly-shaded lamp, and placed another small log on the hearth, with a pine cone behind it, I left the room to see if my lady's tea was ready. Close outside the door I met Janet, the under-housemaid.</p>
        <p>"Why, Miss Howard," she said, "I thought you were never never a-coming out, and I didn't lA* to knock at my lady's door." "To be sure not," I replied quietky, "unless it were or consequence."</p>
        <p>"It is of consequence," replied the girl. whose pleasant, merry face seemed full of eagerness, what about I could not tell, for ours was a cruet humdrum household. "It is of consequence, and they want you home at once." "Want me home!" I exclaimed, "in this rain, and at my lady's tea-time. Is anyone ill?" "Oh, no," replied Janet laughing. "Your sister Miriam seemed a bit like going crazy, but no one's ill. It's great news about something, Miss Howard, and you're to go at once, Miriam said, whether my lady likes it or not," and she gave me a merry glance as she tossed her comely head saucily. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>But-I was vexed to hear her speak so, and only replied: "Inde.d. 11,7 must wait my lady's convenience " ; and Thomas appearing then with the tea-tray I epened the studio door for him and followed him in. It was not till after the dinner-bell rang that I could get off to run down to the village and learn what it was that had excited Miriam so much.</p>
        <p>The rain had stopped, but the sky was black and lowering still, and I stepped into more than one great puddle as I ran down the dark village street to the ivy-covered cottage, where father and mother lived with Miriam ancfSam ; the only two left at home. Paul, who was twenty-two, had been more than a year as footman at the Rectory. Father was cashier at the brewery; a quiet, some- what delicate man, fond of his book, and passionately fond of his old violin, when he could get away by himself any time. Sam, a slim lad of eighteen, was his clerk and Miriam helped mother at home—when she did not leave mother to do everything and go gadding out with other giddy girls of her own age. The cottage was lighted up like the Chase, and keen though I was to hear the news, I could not but stop and admire the great white and yellow chrysanthemums that glittered with rain-drops in the light streaming from our parlour window. It was like a little babel when I went in. Supper was laid out in the parlour, and mother sat in her best grey alpeci, with her hands folded on her black silk apron, talking fast to Paul, who seemed deeply excited. Miriam, decked out in pink ribbons, was talking nineteen to the dozen with Sam. Father alone was quiet and looking, I thought, a trifle sad and bewildered. Sam and Miriam bounded up when I entered. "What a time you've been. Why didn't you come sooner? We are rich people, Racael. We've had a fortune left us."</p>
        <p>They hurled their sentences at me in a way that nearly knocked me over. - -</p>
        <p>I s-at down by father and begged them to be quiet, and after a while 1 gathered out of the tumult that father's twin brother, who had gone fifteen years beton to America, was dead, and had left fatter a lot of dollars.</p>
        <p>I thought I did not care for money, but this unexpected news stirred me very deeply. Like every new experience it widened my horizon immeasurably. It made the impossible possible, placed the %awe:tellable within my band. I had believed I did not value money, or what money made possible—that I was content to wait until I could grasp more lasting powers. But this news made my heart beat and my lips smile. I was sadly disappointed with myself, but I ate the good supper mother and Miriam had provided. I even tasted Sam's cheap champagne, and laughed with Miriam.</p>
        <p>"We shall have champagne every day now, shan't we father?" she asked. "You will go to school, miss," said Sam, "and have bread and scrape and learn to behave." Whereon Miriatn's face fell, and she said anxiously: "Well, I suppose I shall have to learn a few things if I'm to go into society. I'm only six- teen. • It's not too late to go to a finishing school, though "Learning is never done with," said father, "never."</p>
        <p>"Shall you keep a carriage, father ? " And we all laughed again. Fancy father and mother driving about in a carriage like the Rector and his wife. Yet, why not? Uncle Tom had left a lot of money—a hundred thousand pounds, I think father said—quite enough to keep a citrriage, and father came of an old stock, and looked like a gentleman with his clear-cut features, and fine musician's hands.</p>
        <p>"I shall divide the money out equally, children,• said father before we parted. "'Tis too much for any one man to keep. I shall give you each a good share now; your mother and I have agreed to that. I don't want you to be waiting for my death, though, ,naybe, you'll get some more then. And I shall give a helping hand to many that we know need it. You will, neither of you, be against that ? "</p>
        <p>Paul walked home with me before he cut across the park to the Rectory. He had been rather silent all the evening, though his dark, thoughtful eyes shone with sup- pressed excitement. He was a handsome, straight fellow, with father's features, and with brown hair that never would lie flat and straight as its owner ofttimes tried to make it, but followed its own fancy, regardless of Paul's position in the world.</p>
        <p>We had wanted Paul to be a schoolmaster, for he was much given to books and study, and as a lad had been always in the choir, and had even learnt to play the organ a little. Nothing, how- ever, would please Paul but going to the Rectory. We thought it beneath him to give up his sehool work and go as a footman; for though I might be said to be in service myself, I was treated by my lady more as a companion and was never expected to join the party in the servants' hall, and it was only the female servants that I had known all my life who called me by my Christian name; to all the men in the house and on the estate I was "Miss Froward."</p>
        <p>But Paul was devoted to the Rectory; and he chose rather to be a footman there than a grade higher anywhere else. We walked some yards in silence. My mind was busy with many thoughts, and I scarce knew where or how I stepped. Presently Paul's hand slipped through my arm, and his nervous grasp shewed me that he was strongly moved. "It is wonderful, Paul," I said. „_ .</p>
        <p>"I don't know whether I shall keep my senses, Rachel," he replied. "Why, Paul? Does it excite you so greatly?" "It may enable me to realise the dream of my life," he said, almost in a whisper. "Why, Paul, I never fancied you cared for anything." "I am not given to talking," he replied, "you know that. But since I was a little lad in the choir Miss Irma has been my star of stars." "Miss Irma!" I gasped. "0 Paul!" Miss Irma! The idol of the Rectory, the lovely spoilt, pet of the Rev. Cyril Bathurst, and his wife, their only child, for whom a Prince of the blood royal wou'd hardly be good enough. Had too mu. .1 study made Paul mad ? "I would have served her like a slave in faithfu silence my life through," he went on. "No one not even you, should ever have known the dreams that were in my heart. I have lived for WWI years</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHAPTER IL</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IL</p>
        <p>When I entered the studio I found Mr. Gerald Dudley with his mother. He was the only child at the Chnse, as Miss Irma was at the Rectory. He was Lieutenant in a regiment quartered at Hounslow, and was ofttn at home for • a day or two on leave. He was a good-looking young fellow, with fairish hair and a military moustache, not by any means as handsome as our Paul; but then, he would be a Baronet in time, and a Baronet does not need to be so prepossessing as his inferiors.</p>
        <p>"I have been trying to amuse my mother in your absence, Mistress Rachel," he said pleasantly, as I entered, and he pointed to the little table betide Lady Isabella's sofa, on which several jewel-cases lay open, the brilliants within them reflecting the fire and lamp in a myriad shades,</p>
        <p>"How they glitter," I exclaimed, "they quite make my eyes ache."</p>
        <p>"Take them away, Rachel, please," said Lady Isabella, "and lock them up. Mr. Gerald says they ought to.be reset, but I cannot talk about it to-night. What is this foolish gossip about you in the ?" she added, turning her 'dark eyes, languid with pain, straight upon me. "Mr. Gerald says you have had a fortune left you." Mr. Gerald paused in his task of snapping jewel-cases, and looked at me with inquiring interea.. "Father has had some money left to him," I replied diffidently. I did not want to boast, or to exult, neither did I wish to appear ashamed of this luck, which, after all, belonged to my life, and had been pre-arranged, probably, before my advent on earth.</p>
        <p>"Then you will be leaving me, I suppose, Rachel?" exclaimed Lady Isabella. Her troubled eyes followed me restlessly, as I locked away the jewel-cases in a bureau, and straightened one or two matters that offended my sense of order. She had been a very handsome woman in her younger days, tall and stately, and she still retained her winsome dark eyes and the soft silky black hair of her girlhood. It was not over-abundant hair, but it lay in soft natural rings about her neck and temples, and went far to soften a complexion which had become somewhat florid, and a neck that nad grown decidedly fat.</p>
        <p>"Your voice is soothing, and you do not gabble when you read to me," continued Lady Isabella in a plaintive tone, as she swayed one shapely silk- encased foot, from which the slipper had dropped, to and fro, with the restless movement caused by unceasing pain, "and you do not ask me every five minutes if I feel better," and Lady Isabella laughed a little laugh that was half hysterical and wholly pathetic. "I shall not wish to leave you," I said, "I am very happy here."</p>
        <p>"You say so now, but we shall see presently," paid Lady Isabella, rising languidly from the sofa and putting on the slipper that had dropped. "Fortunately, you are quite unlike most girls; but we shall see. Good-night, Gerald. Will you read to me a little while, Eachel ? I almost think I shall sleep to-night." And she did. I lowered my voice by slow degrees; my reading grew gradually fainter and fainter, until I ceased altogether, and she still slept on. If I had stopped suddenly it would I,a-se awakened her at once,and I was so glad to see her sleep. She passed so many hours in suffering, that I always rejoiced to see her drop asleep. She wee very good to me; she liked me, and I liked her; but I had sometimes heard her speak very sharply to Harris, who was her lady's-maid, and had been with her for years. But who could blame her? She was so frequently in pain, and she so rarely complained. " I could not sleep muelvthat night. My brain throbbed and jumped with excitement, and .1 was angry that it should. Money could give me nothing that -I cared greatly for, and yet this unexpected fortune had made the still waters of my quiet life to boil and bubble.</p>
        <p>What would father and mother do? Would they wish sue to leave the Chase, and live an idle lady's life? What would Paul do? And my brain throbbed again. Our Paul lifting his thoughts to Miss Irma! Ihe strong hand of custom made me blush hotly at the thought, though a moment later I asked myself the question I.had asked myself in the park: Why not? I went downstairs the next morning feeling very much as if I had been at a smoking concerCover- night. Beside Lady isabeila's studio door was a French window opening on to a large balcony sur- mounting the portico of the front door. I opened it and stepped out into the misty, bright Octotper air. The keen touch of su!iiinn in the atmosphere braced my nerves like a stimulant, the sweet smell of the park after yesterday's rain freshened my appetite. As 1 re-entered the house, Sir George Do,llPy Came briskly along the corridor, and before I chu!d reach the studio.7he had opened the door himself, and stood wailing for me to enter before</p>
        <p>"Gond-morning, Miss noward," he sAid, twink- ling curiously at me through his spectacles. "Good-morning." "Good-morning, Sir George," I managed to reply, though I was so surprised that I nearly measured my length on the floor in front of him by tripping over the thick mat at. the door.</p>
        <p>Evidently I had risen in his estimation. He had heard about the money. It was very humiliating. Humiliating to me. it being my ambition to be vnl-ed for what I was, nut for my financial position. Humiliating to him, as an dicer of the Church militant.</p>
        <p>I wanted people to live up to my ideals, although I entirely failed to do it myself. Sir George Dudley was a squire first, and a clergyman afterwards. A good man according to his lights, which were not brilliant. A man who desired, above all things, to have a conscience void of offence ; who found Biblical authority for all the harmless delights of his life; who took a little wine "for his stomach's sake," and held that all animals were given for the use of man, and that the use of a fox was to amuse men, and'so on. His was a negative life and character; if he did no particular good, he did no particular harm; he helped Mr. Bathurst most, Sundays in the church services, preached occasionally, and once or twice a week took his Bible under his arm and called upon one or two of the more respectable cottagers., who needed no physician, but who appreciated a friendly chat on matters social and religious. Sir George's religion tied him rigidly to the letter; Lady Isabella trxpiently ignored the letter entirely;. nut she rarely, even in her sharpest. suffering, forgot the spirit of the teaching:gi'ven on the Mount of Olives. ,There was no shadow of difference in her manner to me after I became rich. She was uneasy from time to time lest I should leale her; but beyond this, she treated me as she had ever done—with kindness and consideration, never with familiarity. She was rarely familiar with anyone. -•- I was playing to her on my violin just before luncheon, for I had inherited father's passion for the violin, and Lady Isabella loved music, when there was a little impatient tap at the studio door, and before any of us could bid the tapper enter, the door opened, and Miss Irma peeped round it, her bright young face lighted up with smiles, her fair hair tossed about her temples by the autumn wind, and her throat looking dazzlingly white by contrast with her dark green riding-habit and be•tver hat to match.</p>
        <p>I thought I had never seen her look more bewitching, and, remembering what Paul had told me. I looked at her with newly-awakened interest. She came in, lightly bolding her habit in one hand, her blue eyes bright with merriment, and greeted Lady Isabella; then she turned swiftly to me. I had put down my violin, and taken up a rough petticoat I was knitting. "Are you going on knitting, Rachel, just the same?" "Of course," I said; "why not?"</p>
      </div>
