      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>ASSUAN DAM.</head>
        <p>ASSUAN DAM.</p>
        <p>Ike great engineering feat of the damming the Vile at the [First Cataract at the cost of several million pounds is one which is likely to prove a tremendous factor in the future prosperity of Egypt and its internal development. It was only fitting that special recognition of this great ex- ample of British enterprise should be given from the throne itself. Therefore it was very well received when it was announced that the Duke of Connaught would open the Assouan Dam on his way to the Delhi Durbar ; and thisyeek be carried out the programme arranged for him in Egypt amidst a very distinguished company. This dam has been discussed, off and on, for many years, and all that was really wanted were the men to carry it out. These appeared in Sir John Aird, the head of the great firm of contractors, Sir Ernest Cassel, the well-k no w,n banker and financier, and a few other ener- getic men of brains: and the contract w a s made for its com- pletion within five years. Despite_ unf o r-</p>
        <p>DUKE OF CONNAUGHT IN HIS MASONIC NODES.</p>
        <p>seen difficulties, and ratre than a million of unexpected expendi- ture, Sir John Aird, ,who was quite the hero of last Tuesday, and his firm carried out their great undertaliwr and completed it a full year before the scheduled time, a marvellous accomplishinent. This dam will, it is calculated, pay a hundred per cent annually on the outlay in increased pros- perity, as agriculturally it will be of enormous value in storing water, flooding the country and acting as a substitute for the necessary rainfall, which nature has denied Egypt.</p>
        <p>9 /4 .'' sfvo,l,.A -Rx--.,- ,H.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>AFFAIRS IN VENEZUELA.</head>
        <p>AFFAIRS IN VENEZUELA.</p>
        <p>Venezuela of late has been looming on the political horizon disproportionately large, and now matters have come to a head. Internally it has been torn asunder by civil war for some time owing to the misgovernment of President Castro, who seems to be an adventurer of a bad type and an unscrupulous lirebrand. Under General de Mato, the insurgents have been trying to oust him from rower, though hitherto unsuccessfully. But, since he has em- broiled Venezuela abroad, they have redoubled their efforts and appear to have a good chance of ultimate victory, unless President Castro climbs down more quickly than gracefully, and concedes the demands of Britain and Germany, who are making common cause in the matter. The trouble is one of some standing, and refers to the non-payment of interest on invested money,</p>
        <p>PRESIDRRT CASTRO.</p>
        <p>FERMAN VINETA,</p>
        <p>BEITISII ARIADNE.</p>
        <p>THE TWO LAIIG EST SHIPS SENT TO VENEZUELA.</p>
        <p>• the seizure of foreign vessels and railways, the treatment a European inhabitants, and the sum- mary seizure of various properties during the civil war. No att•ntion has been paid to demands for compensation, and Castro has even had the im- pudence to put in a counter-claim against us with reference to the Ban lligh, the vessel which was allowed to leave the Port -of London only under strict guarantees from the Columbian Government. Castro has been relying upon a broad interpreta- tion on the part of America of the oft-quoted Munroe doctrine; but the States show no desire to interfere and Great Britain and Germany have despatched small squadrons (we have sent six ships under Ariadne) to enforce their interests, while 'ltaly is probably sending two. THE ARCHBISHOP OF CANTERBURY.</p>
        <p>The health of the Archbishop of Canterbury has been giving considerable anxiety to his many friends and admirers of late, and it is noticed with the utmost regret that since his recent ill- ness he is not the same fine, virile old man that he was a few months ago. In fact, he has aged almost beyond recognition in a very short time, and though no immediate anxiety is felt about his state of health, he is undoubtedly physically very weak. This, too, is noticeable in his speaking ; his ideas do not seem to flow with the same readi- ness as of old, or with the old sledge hammer force. This was par- ticularly notice- able in the House of Lords last week in his speech on the Education Bill, and his physical collapse caused a very painful sensation, which can only be paralleled by the case of Lord St. Leonards, th e famous lawyer, under similar circumstances, and the prelate's own sudden illness at the Coronation of King Edward. It is said that it is only in his legs that the Primate is weak; but it is painfully noticeable ill many other respects, especially when one recalls the sturdy, upright Dr. Temple of Past days. He has indeed fought the good fight, and he hag always been a great fighter: but the days of his active camp .signing must be drawing to a close. Dr. Temple is widely respected by all sections of re- ligious opinion, however much they may differ from him, and great sympathy is expressed on all sides. Ju: has been the great keynote of his character, ever since the pow historic Rugby boy said that he was "a beast but a just beast." It is to be hoped that tie will be spared for many Wore years a useful if less active work.</p>
        <p>3iice</p>
