      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>SCIENTIFIC TRAWLING.</head>
        <p>SCIENTIFIC TRAWLING.</p>
        <p>annual grant the innumer- our coast, the equipped and make investi-</p>
        <p>The Government having made an of A 5,500 for the purpose of solving able problems of sea fishing around Marine Biological Association have sent out a steamer, the Huxley, to, gations in the North Sea. It was formerly contended that wholesale injury is done to the fisheries through the trawl playing havoc with the .eggs of the fish at the bottom of the sea. Professor Sara, the Norwegian scientist, however, discovered that cod's eggs float, and this led to the same discovery by other observers in reference to the haddock and the whiting; and, finally, it was observed that the eggs of all "food fish," with one exception, are on the surface of the sea.</p>
        <p>The exception is the herring, and this is the more remarkable as the herring is the typical example of surface swimmers. One of the (Inties</p>
        <p>SEA.</p>
        <p>of those on board the investigating boat will be to mark fishes which they catch and then restore them to the sea. The marks are little numbered bone discs, and thus any fish henceforward caught with a disc attached will bear with him a record of and a reference to his probable movements. Among the biologists who have undertaken work on the Huxley are Mr. R. A. Todd, Mr. Balfour Brown, Mr. C. F. Cooper, and Mr. H. M. Kyle, who will examine invertebrates. Other boats are to be sent out but the range of the English scientific trawler may be said to be from Horn Reef to Texel.</p>
