      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>SELF-WILLED VICAR.</head>
        <p>A SELF-WILLED VICAR.</p>
        <p>The Rev. M. M. Evans, vicar of the church of St. Michael, Finsbury, London, has been exciting considerable interest in religious circles. Mr. Evans has been practising extra-liturgical services—services outside the scope and meaning of the Prayer-book—and persisted in these practices in spite of the Bishop. For some time, the church and its vicar have been under the ban of episcopal authority, the Bishop of London having signified his intention not to preach or confirm in the church until the Archbishop's judgment on reservation given in May 1900 was obeyed. The Bishop even went further than this. The adoption of the Roman Catholic office of Bene- diction, the reci- tation of the Rosary, and the Devotion to the Sacred Heart led him to inti- mate that unless these were surren- dered he would institute proceed- ings under the Church Discip- line Act of 1840.</p>
        <p>THE BEV. I. M. EVANS.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Evans did not obey the episcopal admonition until threatened with a commission of inquiry which could have dealt with the charges under the Act. If the commissioners had reported that there was snificient prima facie ground for' instituting further proceedings the Bishop would have then had the power to proncunce sentence. The vicar has been in charge of St. Michael's for eleven years, and has built up a large and devotel congregation, which, poor as it is, controoates £250 a year, mostly in coppers, to the church fund.</p>
