      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>AN ANCIENT FLEET STREET HOUSE.</head>
        <p>AN ANCIENT FLEET STREET HOUSE.</p>
        <p>There is an interesting old building in Fleet Street, which by some is said to have been the palace of Henry VIII. and Cardinal Wolsey. This house has been used as a hairdresser's premises for the past forty years, but now the London County Council has bought the freehold and it has been decided to restore axed preserve the ancient pile.</p>
        <p>TIIE 'HOUSE AS IT WILL APrEAR WHEN RESTORED.</p>
        <p>The prirent tenant, sonic years ago, hung a heavy lamp fioin the building on the first floor, and, to 1-,u 4,FLlpport for it, • lie front was pierced in Beards. or 4 beam. It was then discovered that the front to thit street was a false one, masking the original, which hail carved oak pilasters. . A County Conned architect took the matter up, and, at Sir John Soane's Museum, Lincoln's Inn 'Fields, had the good fortune to discover a print this house in its first condition. The County Council intend to remove the familiar false front and restore the building to its original appestrawe as presented in the old print.</p>
