      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>FILL E:i E , FHIDAY, DECEMBER 12, 1902.</head>
        <p>FILL E:i E , FHIDAY, DECEMBER 12, 1902.</p>
        <p>• HULL KINGSTON ROVERS v. ST. HELENS. 1 Owing to the frost the ground at Craven-street was barely fit for play when St. Helens kicked off. The Rovers attacked vigorously and opened the scoring with a try by Phipps. From the next scrummage the Hull forwards took the ball away in grand style and Gath picking it up smartly beat all comers, and planted it over the line. In both cases Starks failed to add the goal, and at half-time the Hull team led by two tries to nil. When play was resumed it was the general impression that the Rovers, who looked so much the heavier team, would pile up a big score; but the defence of their opponents was plucky and successful, and more ' than once they came within an ace of scoring. Two , goals by Gordon brought the result—Kingston Rovers 2 goals 2 tries (10 points) to St. Helens nil. Teams : St. klelens.—Foulkes, back; Liversedge, Wearing, J. Creevey, and C. Creevey, three-quarter backs • Kennedy and Banks, half backs; W. Briers, Savage, Thompson G Mawson, reen, Whiteley, Mavitty, and Prescott, for-, wards. Hull Kingston Rovers.—H. Sinclair, back; W Phipps, : J. Gordon. R. Wilson, and G. Bell, three-quarter backs • J. Gath and W. Guy, half backs; A. Starks, A. Kemp, G. Fletcher, W Feetham, A. Windle, F. Gorman, J Picker- ing„ and R. Golding. forwards. Mr. J. Bruckshaw (Stock indges, 1</p>
        <p>)ort); touch</p>
        <p>Referee,Mr. J. Brae&amp; „- .n j_ Messrs. W. Bloor (Norminton), and W. Jack-son, (Hamlet.)</p>
        <p>HALIFAX. v. LEIGH. About 5,000 spectators witnessed this game at:Hanson- lane, Halifax. Johnson started for the visitors. Leigh were soon afterwards penalised, and Little kicked a goal. Hinson, Pearson, and Bennett enabled Leigh to attack. , Johnson took a penalty kick, but the ball struck the upright, and bounded back into play. Halifaxstavedoff further attack, and for several minutes the Leigh goal was in danger. Little kicked up the field, and Ganley seemed to have plenty of time to touch down. Before he was able to make the ball dead, however Joe Riley came up, and scored a try. Little failed at goal. Leigh, in the early part of the second half, played well, but they were ultimately driven back. A series of short sharp passes by the Ihome forwards nearly ended disastrously for the visitors, but the danger was only temporarily averted. Fromone of the succeed- 1 ing scrummages Brown passed quickly to Rigg, who darted over in a good position, and Little kicked a goal. Halifax continued to hold the upper hand, and Nettle- ton ran over, no goal resulting from Little's kick. The! visitors rallied just before the finish, and Johnson just missed kicking apenalty goal. Result :—Halifax 2 goals and 3 tries (13 points), Leigh nil. BRADFORD v. SWINTON : BROADLEY'S I</p>
      </div>
