      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>BENEFIT.</head>
        <p>BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>This match was set apart as a benefit for Tom Broad- ley, the popular captain of the Bradford team, and a crowd of about 8,000 attended to witness the game.—ln the first half the visitors generally got possession, but the work of their backs was completely spoiled by the keen tackling of the Bradford defence. Preston from a place and Wallwork with a drop just missed the goal. Then Bradford took up the pressure,i and close on half- time Broadley picked up n the centre and passed to Eagers. The latter threw back to Broadley, who scored an excellent try, from which Mosby kicked a goal. Half-time —Bradford,: 1 goal and 1 try (5 points) ; Swinton, nil.—On the change of ends Bradford at once assumed the upper hand, and Chorley misfielding,Sinton gathered the ball and ran in a .try unopposed. Mosby again landed the goal. The balance of play was in Brad- ford's favour, the final score being :—Bradford, 2 goals and 2 tries (10 points), Swinton. nil.</p>
        <p>_ HUNSLET V. BRIGHOUSE. By the aid of a straw protection and the use of the Corporation steam roller on the Brighouse ground it was possible to bring this match to an issue. Both teams were strong, and during the first half a capital struggle was seen, in which, however, the long kicking of the Hunslet back division and their well-judged use of touch, coupled with consistently good scrummaging and wheel- ing by the front rank enabled the Parksiders to keep the upper hand. The only scores made were both dropped goals from the field of play by A. Goldthorpe,the second of these points being an especially fine bit of work. Hunslet 2 goals (4 points), Brighouse Rangers nil..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Why Do Double</head>
        <p>Why Do Double</p>
        <p>work on wash-day, or have it done? Fels-Naptha soap saves half the rubbing and half the wear on clothes.</p>
        <p>If you don't think so, your grocer returns your money in full. a bar. 2 FELS-NAPTILA 39 Wilson Street London EC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>HOWLEY UNITED V. STOCKTON HBA'TIL ALBIONS.— At Stockton Heath. In the first half the Albions had much the better of the play, but the United defence was sound. Half-time: Albions 2, United nil. In the second half the game was fast. Warburton,Pritohard,Harrison, Rowlinson, and Rowlands increased the score. Result; United 7, Albions 3. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRINITY v. --TiliLwALL.—Played at Warrington. After several attempts had been made, Jones scored for Trinity. Half-time: Warrington Trinity, one goal, Thelwall, nil. The second half was a little more exciting. Thelwall began to gain the upper hand, their forwards making good combined efforts to equal- ize. Hawazd put in three shots in quick succession, which Potter fisted out, but the fourth proved to be too much forhim, Result: Trinity, one goal; Thelwall, ' one goal. LATCHFORD V. LATCHFORD BRITANNIA.—PIayed on the ground of the former. The visitors 'commenced hostilities and at once made tracks for the home goal; and after some neat work by the forwards, Blackshaw beat Melton, the home custodian, with a long shot. Eventually some smart play by Kenyon and Candle nearly let the locals in, but Hamblett cleared nicely. Newall next got possession on the right wing, and centred to Burton, who added a second goal. Latchford were still kept on the defensive, when Britannia wer e awarded a penalty and T Wright taking the kick, beat Melton with a swift shot. At half-time, Latchford Britannia were leading by three goals to nil, In the second half the Latchfordians were soon hovering round the visitors' goal. The backs relieved in fine style, but the locals again attacked and from a corner kick, Tinkler scored an easy goal. Play had only been in progress a few minutes when Ashton, for Britannia, beat the home custodian with a splendid shot. Even play then followed. Final result :—Latchford Britian ia , four goals; Latchford, one goal. SECOND DIVISION.) WILDERSPOOL (SECOND V. WARRINGTON ALBIONS.— This match was played at Arpley last Saturday before a big crowd. Webb started for the Albions before a slight wind and the home forwards became very dangerous on several occasions. C. Turner being called upon to save which he did in grand style. The venue was changed and the visitors took the ball down to the home goal which resulted in a corner a minor only being registered. Half-time came soon afterwards with a clear sheet. On resuming the visitors livened up and went away with great dash, Webb having hard lines injust kicking over the bar. From the goal kick the home team had little relief Walton putting in a good centre, which resulted in Corradine scoring. The Central went down with a rush and one of the forwards put in a good shot. Turner fisted out and relieved the visitors. Full time arrived soon afterwards,the score standing: NVa.rrington Alb ions 1 goal, Wilderspool Central nil. FRIENDLY MATCH. LATCHFORD CHRIST CHURCH CHOIR BOYS V. WAR- RINGTON GRAAIMAR SCHOOL—Played OR the ground of the latter team. Banks kicked off for the Choir, against the wind, and Ferguson was prominent with is fine dribble. Latchford pressed and Dawson receiving, this player scored. Half-time: Choir, one goal; Grammar School, nil. After the restart, A. Clarke scored another goal. Watkins (for the school) sent in a good shot, but Unsworth's defence was sure, and he sent the ball down the field to Fairclough, who scored the Choir's third goal. The Choir continued to have the best of play,and add ng three more goals, won by six goals to nil.</p>
      </div>
