      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Borax people seem to pass all their days in eon. gnus' expectation of the unexpected. Yocum men try to air their knowledge ; old men try to avoid airing their ignorance. NEXT to real musical culture is the ability to seem delighted when you are bored. Two consensus of opinion is that it will be s cold day when the North Pole is discovered. JOHNNY: "My father's a policeman ; what does your father do?" Jimmy: • W hat ma tells him." A Foos man is discontented because he can't get what he wants—a rich man because he can't want what he gets. To obtain the very best results, bread which is cast upon the waters should not be too old to make Wiling of. A PRUDENT Mir.—Brown: "My dear fellow —two umbrellas ! What on earth is that for ? " Jones "Why, in cue I leave one anywhere." Talc probability is that things are neither as black as they are painted, nor as white as they are whitewashed. WREN some people assume to be pleasant, they act as it they had a disagreeable but unavoidable duty to perform. Jr the "sultry day in August" and the "bleak dav in December" were omitted from our litera- ture there would be many a gaping void. tilt!: (having nothing else to say): "It's funny Imw we ever °Mlle to think so moon of each other." He: " Funny ? It's positively ridi-</p>
        <p>culouls." GRANDMA: "Ali, my dear, the men are not what they were fifty years ago." Ethel: "Well, granny, you know fifty years will change any man." Two WAYS OF LOOKING AT IT.—Artist (pointing to stranded boat) "Beautiful effect, with that old boat in the foreground." "Old Salt: " Yoe she'e just in a good position to give her a fresh. web or tar." FARMER HAYRICK : goin' ter give Jim as good an eddieation as money can buy." Farmer Cornbin "What college have ye got in mind?" Fet mer Hayrick: "Well, Sarah an' me heir pitched on ElAtn, cuz the name sounds as if he'd git plenty of gsub." .Nl5l. BLINKER (to applicant for his daughter's' hand) : "is your position sound?" Applicant: Well, I should smile. I'm a trombone player." THAT SETTLED IT. "I thought you said, Grumpy, that you would never allow your wife to ride a bike?" "So, I did, but the happened to hear of it." A BOY of fifteen thinks he is ton old to run errands, but after he is twenty-five and married, he begins again. AN affectionate Irishman once enlisted in the 75th Regiment in order to be near his brother, who was a corporal in the MIL STILL RETAINED THAT.—BiII Yards: "That man Williams never lost his head in a football match Jet, did he?" G. Olpher "No, I think not. He's lost an ear, part of his nose, and eight teeth, but I do not remembsr ever hearing of him losing his head." "WHAT'S the matter, Tommy?" "The boy across the street hit me." "Oh, well, I wouldn't cry for that." "Show that you can be a little man." "I ain't crying for that." "Then what are you crying for ?"` He ran into the house be- fore I could get at him." "Do you hetieve in the power of the human eye with a wild beast?" asked Gahleigh. "Yee, said the professor, "the power of the eye is very useful—to see the vv,l,t beast coining." A MAN advertised for the worst dog in the city. Hifi idea was that the people were so fond of their own dogs that they wouldn't bring a single animal to him. But he lost his but. „Thousands called,* each bringing his neighbour's dog. "A MAN,' observed the student of social phe- nomena," "is never satisfied so Ion,: as there is anything he wants; a woman is never satisfied so long as there is anything sne can get." FllthT LAWYER: "Bags was in that train when the accident occurred. He showed remarkable preseno of mind." Second Lawyer: " How ?" First Lawyer: "Within ten minutes after the col- lision he had himself retained to bring a dozen &amp;triage subs against the company." "I have been looking for you all the evening," he said, as he approached her in the conservatory; "I want you to be my partner—" "This is so sudden," she gasped. "--at whist," he con- cluded. And now there is a coldness between them. " BESSIE," said Adolphus to his sister, "I have taken a fancy to a youag lady with whom I am but slightly aequainted, and would like to know what her faults are. How can find out?" "Praise her to her young lady acquaintances I" said Bessie. SMART YOUTH (to rustics old party on opposite tide of railway carriage): "You seem to be looking at me pretty closely. Do I remind you of some- body you used to know?" Rustic Old Party: " Yes ; you remind me of an aunt of mine in Whiteohapel, only She's got a little more mous- tache than you have." "My wife is the most ingenious woman who ever lived," said Kipper. "I believe you," re- turned Nipper, politely. "But you don't know why you believe me," intimated Kipper. "To tell the truth, I don't," replied Nipper, looking bored. "Weil, I'll tell you. We've been married twelve years and lived in the same house all the time, and this morning she found a new place in which to hide my slippers." Tim mania for giving salesmen a lot of trouble before making a purchase was recently carried into an ironmonger's shop. As the salesman tells the story s young woman entered and said she wanted a two-foot rule for her father. He showed her every rnle in the place, and was dumfounded when the girl said: "Haven't you got any longer two-foot rules than these?" No AUTHORlTY.—Housemaid (while out for a stroll) : Jaae, which is the properest way te carry this 'ere violin oase—the thick or the narrer hand foremost?" Cook (whilst shifting sketch- book from oue hand to the other) : " Lor, Susan, 'ow cam I tail? Yer doesn't suppose that I'm a welkin' hook ot hettyket, does yer ? " " MARIA," said Mr. Welkerston, "I don't understand how you can bring yourself to wear hair that has belonged to some other person. Ceti! It riiskes me feel creepy." "Well," she replied, as she deftly lifted a long brown hair from hi, CAIL sleeve, "this doesn't seem to have given you the creep. I suppose you got it in the crowded tsain coming home, didn't you?" ABOUT lisviaN•rioils —Old Friend: "Well, how is yoor dying machine getting along? " Inventor: " Getting along I I finished that twenty years regs. E.ery detail is complete. There it a4i.n Is, re aily to go." "Why don't you show it to tire world ?" " Can't. All men are fools." " Wha f'f4 the matter?" "Can't find a man any- where with sense enough to climb up a steeple and try it." . 'lnc louse Racron (in evident embarrassment) : "My deAa Mies Clara, I"—trying to leave hie chair—" I bAitsve I have formed an—attachment, and--" Miss Clara (blushing furiously): "Oh, Arthur—l hisan NIL% Greene—this is so unexpected. I must----" The Young Rector (frantically) : par•don, tilts Clara; but I was about to say I base formed an attachment for this chair, due to the presence of a bit of cobbler's wax placed here by that unregenerate brother of yours.' (Intense 1 defight el the small boy in ambush.) "ARE you a dancing master?" he asked, as he came hare the room with that rolling stride which life on the ocean wave causes. "Yes," answered the professor. "Can you sing?" "A little." " Wsil, I've been so busy all my life working aboard ship that I never had time to learn any accomplishments. I've got some money now, and I want you to teach me how to dance the sailor's hornpipe and sing Nancy Lee' like they do on the stage, so that I won't have any more trouble in convincing people that I'm the real thing." AT first the person in the tattered shawl shrank timidly away • but something in the face of the prand dame bade her be of good courage. "Pity !" the !altered, extending her trembling hand. The maid of luxury contemplated the picture of abject poverty, and her heart softened. " Here !" she exclaimed, impulsively opening her purse and tak- ing from it two recipes for pudding and a news- paper poem, "Heaven bless you " gasped the beggar, scarcely able to believe her senses. For, after all, she was a woman, with a woman's notices , a the value of those things.</p>
