      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Roman Remains at Warrington.</head>
        <p>Roman Remains at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the ninth ordinary meeting rf the Historic. Society of Lancashire and Cheshire Mr. Thomas May F.E.1.5., read a paper on the "Discoveries at Vera tinum in 1901-02. The chair was occupied by Mr G. T. Shaw. After the usual business Mr. May com- menced by showing the position of Veratinum on the great North Road, and its connection with other I Roman stations in Lancashire and Cheshire, anct giving a review of the great amount of work accom- , plished with the most satisfactory results. He al.*, described the iron, glass, pottery, classical figures in bronze, arrow heads in iron, and many smaller objects, cinerary urns, and the bones of various animals, all illustrated by a series of lantern slides. After a short discussion, in which all were agreed that Veratimart was a manufacturing centre in Roman times, a vote of thanks, proposed by Mr. R. I. Elliott, and seconded by Mr. S. W. Phipp, was accorded to Mr. May for his acellent paper.</p>
