        <head>, , , . . . , . , ,• . – . . . . , . . _. ":144g,f4P'Z''."'LLs.l'_,• •''‘'''''''' I •••(:-...4:1`; ':,'..flZ'•. _. ,"- ,1.,• _ • '• ' ' ' -- ‘ • .___ • ...' .;_:_—___ ___•_.l_.%, . .4'' gl',M;Sq.l.'------.•• •' „" • ' • • . , . _ . . , .</head>
        <p>, , , . . . , . , ,• . - . . . . , . . _. ":144g,f4P'Z''."'LLs.l'_,• •''‘'''''''' I •••(:-...4:1`; ':,'..flZ'•. _. ,"- ,1.,• _ • '• ' ' ' -- ‘ • .___ • ...' .;_:_—___ ___•_.l_.%, . .4'' gl',M;Sq.l.'------.•• •' „" • ' • • . , . _ . . , .</p>
        <p>F'A.T:r?;fs</p>
        <p>through Parliament until the House of Lords is wither mended or ended, or, as history reminds us, frightened oat of its wits. Acquiescence in almost any Measure is possible, and, indeed, easy, where there is a probability of repeal on a change of parties or in consequence of a strong manifesta- tion of public opinion. The people of Massachu- setts might have acquiesced in the tea duty if they had foreseen its speedy repeal. As they foresaw no such thing there ensued the revolutionary war, and the loss of our American Oolocies. If the Nonconformists had not the House of Lords in front they would give their acquiescence to the Education Bill with cheerfulness, appealing to public opinion only, and confideut that whenever public opinion is in a position to express itself through the polls it will be found to be on their side. But the Government has made even the most temporary acquiescence impossible by putting this question of the education of the people's ,children out of popular reach. That is part of the plot; that is one of the assumptions—the most dangerous of the assumptions—on which the Education Bill is based. It is this consideration which made Dr. °farrow) say at Spencer House that the passing of the Bill among other things will endanger the House ot Lords. This is really and seriously the case. The House of Lords has pulled through some tight places of late years, and it has in consequence grown more confident and self-assertive, but the old conditions of its exist- ence remain unchanged. The DUKE OF WEL- INGTON, who was a more far-seeing statesman, than he is generally credited with being, said tg If the House of Lords goes it will go in a storm." The present Government has sown just that particular wind which may be adequate to the end. It is of the sort which comes back as a whirlwind. If the House of Lords stands resolutely in the way of a reciress of the great and carefully-calculated wrong which is done by the Education Bill it will go in a storm. The Church, as an establishment, is likely enough to go along with it. This is what DR. PERCEVAL, the Bishop of Hereford, forsees. He is, of course, a onthnsiastic a Churchman as the other Bishops. But he is concerned lest popular reientment may be aroused against tlae Church. The Bill deals to the Church, he says, an insidious blow. This is undoubtedly the case, but the effects of the blow would be long delayed if the Nonconformists acquiesced, as the " Times " advises, in the Educa- tion Bill. Acquiescence in this case means acquiescence in their own overthrow.</p>
        <p>There are special reasons this year • which make it very desirable that the charitable appeals so numerous at this season,should not be disregarded by those to whom they are:addressed. The plight of many Reservists and other discharged soldiers has been describe•i at length in the newspapers, and, though there may be cases in which the men themselves are mainly to blame, it is not to be denied that undeserved misery exists. Then, of course, the engagement of returned soldiers by many patriotic employers, commendable as it is in itself, reacts naturally upon civilian employment, and helps to congest the supply of labour. Again, the cessation of the stimulus which was given to several trades by the continuance of the war. is now beginning to be felt severely At Government and other factories slackness of work is beginning to tell, and wholesale dis- charges of workmen are heard of; while employment at half-time, which is almost as disastrous for men wife families earning no more than 255. or 28s. a week, may well reduce the half-starved worker into the lethargy of despair,or drive him into acts of pilfering and other petty crimes. We have hail already one or two samples of what the winter may be, and, of course, two or three weeks of frost and snow would add greatly to the number a those on the wrong side of thq line of positive want. Socialists sneer at soup kitchens as a mete" salve for the conscience of the well-to-do," but for the children they are a blessed institution; even the Christmas dinner is not to be despised, whether for the intrinsic worth of beef and plum-pudding or the message of sympathy conveyed from chun to class. At Christmastide kinship andfacquaintance are recalled; now,before the Christmas bells ring out, is the time to re- member the humble and the dependent.</p>
        <p>The wonderful facility which the natives of the Continent of Africa, especially those in South Africa, have for the transmission of news is one of the most puzzling problems of the day. Time after time during the recent campaigning has the fact been proved that news of battles and en,gagemeuts has been transmitted many miles, sometimes hundreds of miles, in a few minutes by the natives without any visible means or any articulate sound. The general facts of many an engagement in the Cape' Orange River, and even Transvaal Colonies were known at Capetown long before the official news had come over the wires, through the natives; and the Basutos on more than one occasion were able to inform the English residents of events which had happened over the border many miles distant within a few minutes of the actual occur- rences. One of the most marvellous examples of this power of transmission, is, perhaps, that of the spread of the news of the British defeats in Natal among the native populations in the Nile Valley which created a situation of unrest in the latter -days of 1899, and which looked at one time very like the herald of a general rising among the natives. It is even said that reports of the defeat of English arms were in circulation in Kaartourn, through the natives, before the official announce- ments had gone through. Whether that be so or not, it is quite certain that, despite the most -elaborate precautions taken with the object of pre- venting the circulation of the evil tidings, the general facts were known among the natives, and had a certain effect in exciting them. People at home are, perhaps, inclined to scoff at the sugges- tion of a power unknown to civilisation, and exceeding in its wonderfal results the Marconi invention, just as they "pooh-pooh " the almost miraculous power of the Kaffir "doctors," but those who are acquainted with the country and the natives affirm the absolute truth of the state- appear so impossible. meats which in this country</p>
      </div>
