        <p>A WARRINGTON CASE.-RCOMMENDED BY A BOY AT THE STATION.</p>
        <p>35, Huline-street, Dalton Bank, Warrington. Mr. Mellor, Dear Sir, —Allow me to tender rny sincere thanks to you for the great benefit. I received whilst under your treatment with my eyes. I was recommended to you by a little boy at the station, lie saw my eyes in bandages, and out of pure sympathy and a knowledge of what y..UI hid done fti others, mentio ci your name to me. I came and after three (3) visits, was able to ees again. You are at libertytu do with this as you think proper.—Youra truly, THOM AS FrukuorAirox, A WARRINGTON CASE DISCHARGED INCURABLE FROM THE EYE HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>20, Leonard Street, Warrington. March sth. 1897. Mr 'Sailor, Dear Sir,—l write to thank you for the marvellous cure you have made of my eyesight. Previous to being recommended t,o you I had been a patient to the Manchester eye Hospital for over two years, and was discharged Incurable. My left eye was completely blind, but alter receiving your treatment for about aeven mouths, my eyesight is restored, and I can see better than ever. I shall be pleased to recommend your treatment to every body that needs a cure,— I am, yours einem*. (511tb.) W. SHRLDOJN.</p>
