      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>DECEMMIR 6tb,1902.</head>
        <p>DECEMMIR 6tb,1902.</p>
        <p>hLeo r6d LORD ROSEBERY'S speech TRi mo seesb "e rayn. d to the Nonconformist deputation has, of course, exposed him to a homily from the "Times.' Possibly this may be followed by a pamphleteering assault on the part of Mr. BALFOUR. That, how- ever, is doubtful, and in any case Lord ROSEBERY is likely to remain unruffled, for what he said to the representatives of the Free Church Council though" the words were strong," as in the case of an ill-used people mentioned by the late Poet LAUREATE, was confined within the strict bounds of the proprieties. The "Times" affects to assume that Lord ROSEBERY in an indirect way recommended the deputation to fight the new legislation by refusing to pay rates. He did nothing of the kind, of course. He expressly said that this method of op- position is one which he does not advise. Bat he did not take the further step of condemning it which is why he is pursued by the moral reproba- tion of the "Times." The truth is that LORD ROSEBERY recognises in the first place that the Nonconformists have a grievance against the Bill which goes far deeper than any objection of his own ; and insthe second place that this grievance is Put out of the reach of reineiiy by the fact that the House of Lords interposes itself between public opinion and the repeal or amendment of obj ictiou- able laws. Tee "Times" has a convenient plati- tude at its command. It is to tile effect that when a measure has been carried through Parlia ment those who dislike it and have opposed it will generally acquiesce when it is brought into operation. This is probably the sort of reasoning by which the Government has reconciled itself to a course of policy which is ne more respectable than an act of theft. "The storm will pass over, the people will forget, it is ouly a tempest in a teapot; the usual calm will follow after the merely occasional storm. These, or something like these, are the, facile phrases by which the PRIME AlimsTka must have beau deluding himself for some months past L3rd ROSEBERY, though he is not a Nonconformist, sees deeper, and he saw in advance the hopelessness of the other " Times" formula, that those who object to legislation may continue to denounce it, and if they think fit may endeavour to repeal it when they get a majority. This is a case in which the getting of a majority means nothing. What mast be the weight of a , majority of the HJuse of Commous if it is to Over.. 4mwer —that is to say if it is to frighten— the overwhelming Tory urtjarity la the House of L.irds ? As to the Noucon- formist grievance against the Echmation Bill, there is absolutely no hope of redress</p>
      </div>
