      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>HALTON PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting 7i the ...... Council a precept was issued for £2O for current expenses. The committee appointed at the time of the opening of the Village Hall was approved by the Council, and Mr. Moors appointed chairman. -</p>
        <p>A RUNCORN YOUTH MISSING. Sylvanus Walker, a young man of Percival-lane, Runcorn, a salt boiler, has been missing since Saturday night. For some time he has been suffering from delusions, and he was last seen running alongside the locks of the Bridgewater Canal A portion of the canal has been dragged, but no _trace of the missing man has been found.</p>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY CHURCH SALE OF WORK. In connection with the above church the sale of work was re-opened on Friday. It wa4 reported that the total for the day would amount to £loo.The stalls and stall- holders were as follows :—Fancy work stall, Mrs. Killick, the Misses Fisher, Witmer and Lea, Mrs. Grice, and Mrs. West; plain work stall, Mrs. Annett, Mrs. Hulme, Mrs. Le Couteur, and Mrs. Ennion ; men's stall, the Rev. N. J. Poole, and Messrs. Bayley, A. Savage, Grice, and Edwards; Sunday school stall, the Misses Turner and Annett: choir boys' stall, Mrs. Smith and the Misses Johnson and Burrows; flower stall, Mrs Arthur Killeross' Mrs. Barker, and the Misses Ada Kill- cross and Bete Amiett ; refreshment 0411_4 Mrs. Horse- Miset norSefleldf Mrs. Waterworth, Mrs. Meadow- croft, Mrs. Turner, and the Misses Loretto Horsefleld, Dunbabin, and Jennie Hilda Waterworth, and Elsie Grice. The total amount realized by the sale of work was about £l5O. A FRANK ADMISSION.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, on Thursday, John Egan, a man of the tramp class, hailing from 13arup, was chargedwith loiter- ing in Marsh-street at 1.39 that morning with intent to commit a felony.—Police-constable Clare stated that he watched him for ten minutes trying the doors of private houses and shops. Upon seeing the constable he ran into an entry.—ln answer to the charge of intent to commit a felony, prison replied, " Quite so." Further questioned by Sergeant Blackhall, he said, "1 daresay if I could have found anything good, I would have taken it.—Superintendent Keighley asked for a week's remand, which was granted. 'Y" BRANCH OF BoNc °RN WONINN'S TEMPERANOR ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The monthly meetinao-i the above Society was held on Monday evening in Bethesda Lecture Hall, which was kindly lent for the occasion. Mrs. Baker presided and in her address gave some very interesting facts re- lating to the numerous deaths caused by excessive drinking. Mrs. McArthur of Rook Ferry also addressed the members. She told them of the great amount of good work being done by the members of the Rock Ferry Branch. and of the amount of money the drink traffic costs us. A very good musical programme was rendered and after the singing of the Temperance Doxology several new members were enrolled.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES. [BY A CORRESPONDENE]. The proposed extending of Runcorn's border's to Weston and Halton has been hailed vvith almost universal satisfaction by the Runcorn townsfolk. It is, as yet ,too early to say what the attitude of the Weston and Halton ratepayers will be to the Urban Council's proposal. But, at all events, it will be safe to predict that they will not like, to put it mildly. to have their modest rate sprung up to the Runcorn Standard. It is, however, only fair that Halton and Weston should throw their lot in with Runcorn when it is remembered that they are dependent upon the town for water and gas. The fact is that the interests of the three districts are absolutely identical and there is no reason why one central authority should not do all the work. It is to be hoped that the Parish Councils will fall in with the Urban Council's proposi- tion, and that ere long the necessary negotiations will be brought to a satisfactory termination.</p>
        <p>A case in which rather more than usual interest centred was heard at the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday. A school teacher was summoned by Superin- tendent Eunion for too severely chastising his boy during a drawing lesson. After a long and patient hear- ing of the case the teacher was flned,the bench remarking that the punishment inflicted was much too severe. There will be considerable sympathy with the teacher, for the boy deserved all he got if no more. The breach of discipline was so "marked "that there of necessity had to be "marking "of quite a different nature in order to prevent any recurrence. If all the boys who went to school were of this stamp, and teachers were not allowed to exercise their authority, there would soon be petty riots in galore. And it would actually require Superintendent Ennion and his staff to restore order in the various schools. It must not be fqrgotten that boys are sent to school to learn how to obq, and he who does not obey a perfectly just command can hardly be too severely punished. The teacher in question is to be sympathised with. He is the victim of circumstances, but he may rest assured that though convicted by a magisterial bench, he is acquitted at the bar of that greater tribunal,over which public opinion sits as judge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>FAILURE OF GROCERS AT ST. HUMS.</p>
        <p>Peter and Joseph Gallagher, grocers, of 56, Church- street, St. Helens,appeared on their public examination. with liabilities amounting to £1,710, and net assets £576. Mr. H. L. Riley appeared on their behalf. Peter Gallagher was first examined by the Official Receiver. He stated that he and his brother commenced the busi- ness in August, 1900, having purchased it from the trustee of an assignment: paying £3OO. of which they borrowed £270 from their mother. They knew that a very good business had at one time been done there,and they thought that by working hard and canvassing they could recover it. They failed, however, in this, and had to be content with very small profltsand high wholesale pines for provisions. They also had to do a large credit trade. Mr. E. M. Owen had been appointed trustee, and the examintaion was adjourned to the Bth January, in order that he might investigate the ac- counts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDA V DECEMBER 12, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDA V DECEMBER 12, 1902</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT</p>
        <p>EARLESTOW.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Saturday was an ideal daTio—r football, but the Earles- town ground was not in an ideal condition, consequently the game was not an ideal one. The game was in con- nection with the Rawcliffe Chorley Charity Cup, and the contestants were St. Helens Rees and Earlestown, between whom an exciting game is always assured. On the present Occasion this assurance was, however, mis- placed. There was a fair crowd of spectators who per- formed many kinds of antics to keep themselves warm, but they suffered disappointment when it became known that the Recs. had turned up with 10 men, and Earles- town had only 9, the absentees from the latter being ! Barlow and Parker. This hadthe effect of disorganising the latter team and otherwise weakening it. Fortun- ately they we able to make up the eleven, by including Barnett and Pennington. The former was next to use- less, and the latter played in ordinary boots, and as a consequence was as often laying down as he was on his feet.</p>
        <p>Of course the ground was as slippery for one team as the other, but really Earlestown nearer looked like a winning team. The only goal they got was from a penalty, whilst those obtained by the Rees were earned by good play. Their combination was much superior to the Earlestown lot, and they were more speedy in the attack. Bartley never by any .means shone at centre forward, and he appeared to be afraid of the goal. Leach played a strong game but he is not the Leach of old. The old warhorse Whitehouse was every- where and always in the right place at the right moment whilst Limonci was always safe at centre half. The backs played a strong game, but like other backs they made mistakes.</p>
        <p>Tomorrow Earlestown are at home to Wigan in a league fixture, when it is hoped they will be able to put a full team in the field andlsecure two greatly needed points.</p>
        <p>The School Attendance Committee of the District Council,deserve every credit for recognizing worth, and at the same time encouraging schoolchildren over whose school destinies they are supposed to watch. It is their duty to see that the children attend school, and it is evident they are not slow to reward them when any- thing exceptional happens. Thus it came about that a girl attending the Wargrave school, who had not been absent on one single occasion for 10 years, and a boy and girl from St Mary's R.C. School with an almost equally meritorious record, were the recipients of handsome rewards from the hands of the committee. It is no mean thing to attend school every day in the year, except holidays, for 10 years, and the children certainly deserve all they received.</p>
        <p>The suggestion arose from the Chairman of the Com- mittee, who, ascertaining the facts of the case, at once undertook to afford some recognition to the children, but the Committee thinking it incumbent upon them to support the Chairman, decided to make the children a substantial present, and this was the course they pur- sued. One of the children received a writing desk, and work box combined, another a writing. desk, and the other a lady's companion, with suitable Inscriptions, and as they were exhibited at the various schools, it will be a source of encouragement to the scholars. As a consequence the Committee have set the precedent,and may before long have to repeat this little precedent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>DROWNING MYSTERY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>DROWNING MYSTERY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>About 8-45 on Saturday morning, Joseph Prime, a waterman at Hey Lock on the St. Helens and Sankey Canal, found the body of a woman named Elizabeth Smith, floating on the surface of the water close to Brad- legh Bridge. With the aid of grappling irons the body was removed from the water and information was given to the police, who conveyed it to the Earlestown Mortuary to await identification. THE INQUEST. Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, conducted an inquest at the Town Hail, Earlestown, on Monday night. William Tashingham' a farmer, of Wright's Farm, Burtonwood, was the first witness called. He said he had identified deceased as Elizabeth Smith, who had lived with him as his wife for the past 11 years. He had noticed that for several months past deceased had been dull and distant in her manner. She arose about 7 o'clock on Saturday morning, the fire already being lit. When he got up half an hour later she was missing. He next saw her body at the Earlestown Mortuary on Sunday. morning. The Coroner: Have you ever had any suspicions that she would take away her life? Witness: None at all! She told me about 10 months ago that work had become a drudge to her and she felt , she could not get through it. Was she a single or a married woman? A single woman.</p>
        <p>• Is it a fact that she INlinted you to get married to her and that you would not, and that fact depressed her? No ! I have not depressed her at all. I have not refused to marry her. The Foreman of the jury (Mr. N. Siddeley); Did you ever promise her marriage? Witness: Well we had talked about it but— Coroner: But what?</p>
        <p>Witness: has Wone and left me now! Coroner: Well you had plenty of time before. There was nothing to prevent yo 4 getting married? Nothing. Emma Danville the wife of Peter Danville, farmer of New Barn, Farm, Burtonwood, said she knew deceased very intimately. She had known her for about 11 years. The Coroner: Has she complained to you several times of being low-spirited? Yes. She once said she could do something to herself. When did you last see her alone? A week last Sunday! Did the fact of her not being married to Tashingham trouble her? Yes, it seemed to trouble her a lot. She said she would like to be married to him, and he had promised to marry her but had fixed no definite date. What clothes had she on when picked out of the water? She had on her oldest clothes. She seems to have gone in her working clothes. Joseph Prime the Waterman who diseovered the body, and Sergeant Barrow gave evidence. After it short deliberation the jury returned a verdict of "Found Drowned."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>DRUNK IN CHARGE OF AN ELECTRIC CAR. Heavy Fine Inflicted at Newton</head>
        <p>DRUNK IN CHARGE OF AN ELECTRIC CAR. Heavy Fine Inflicted at Newton</p>
        <p>Dennis O'Brien, of Church-road, Haydock, a driver of an electric car, in the employ of the St. HelensuudDis- trict Tramways Company, was charged, at the Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday, with being drunk whilst in charge of a car. Inspector Palmer, of Ashton, prosecuted for the police, and called Thomes Mowbray, a collier, of Hay- dock, who said that about half-past eleven on Saturday night, the 22nd ult., he saw a tramcar in Church-road, Haydock. The defendant was the driver, and he was intoxicated. Witness saw him fall of the car whilst going to the handles. John Marsh gave similar evidence. William Barker, inspector for the St Helens Tramways Company, said his attention was called to the defendant by the police. He appeared to be very excited and the worse for drink. Witness took charge of the car back to town. George Bradbury, conductor of the car, said the driver as in a staggering condition when he noticed him ome of the passengers complained about him. O'Brien ell off the car. Defendant had driven the car from , town.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. Gillespie) said, that there seemed to be a great deal of laxity in the way the cars were conducted. Constable Thompson said that at 11.30 on the Saturday night (November,22nd) he saw an electric car proceed- ing at a rapid rate along Church-road; Haydock. As the car dashed past, the trolley of the car flew off the wires and brought the guard wire down with it, the oar coming to a standstill at Holly Bank. Witness went up to it and found the driver drunk. He had lost his controlling handle. Barker came up and took charge, and defendant was sent heme. . . .</p>
        <p>Constable Dagger corroborated. Defendant pleaded not guilty, and said that he could not have driven the car with only one handle if he had been drunk. The controller handle had been taken from the car whilst standing at the terminus. The Chairman said that it was one of the worst cases of carelessness and misconduct they had had before them. At all times it is sufficiently dangerous ' in regard to the running of electric cars in the street but when driven at that time of night by a drunken man it was almost tempting danger. The bench were very much surprised that some of the head officials of the St. Helens Tramways Company had not been present to assist the bench in coming to a decision in the matter, and considered that there had been a very great want of courtesy, and it seemed as if they wanted the case to slip over unnoticed. They would fine the defendant the t heaviest penalty possible, .e., 40s and costs.</p>
        <p>SkLE OF DICKENS RETACs.—Messrs. Sotheby's sale on Saturday included the mahogany office table, gas office chair, the .high-back cane chair, and the looking-glass which for many years were in daily use by Charles Dickens in his private office at. 26, Wellington-street, Strand, where he edited "All the year round." These "relics" were given by Charles Dickens's son to the housekeeper, Mrs. Hedderlv, from whom they were bought by the late Henry Walker and removed to the residence of his stepson, James Hooper, of Bromley, Kent, who alone from that time has had the custody of them. They realised £55 (Denham).</p>
        <p>001. D. Colds are unpleasant at all corms. COLDS times and often difficult to COLDS. atADS. get rid of, but an excellent and COLDS. COLDS. agreeable remedy Is a basin of gruel, COLDS. COLDS. taken every night, made from COLDS. COLDS. ROBINSON'S PATENT GROLTS. COLDS TRH DOCTOR'S ENEMY.—A pair of Aix TAYLOR'S Wonderful Boote.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS'</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS'</p>
        <p>Rugby Football. By " Ovalist." It was a great disappointment to Warrington peopi,e to learn on Saturday afternoon that the referee ha' declared the ground too hard for play and that th match with Wigan was, in consequence, abandoned There has not been a finer day this season for foot- ball than was Saturday, the conditions being such as suited players and spectators alike. The sun shone brightly, and there was a frosty sharpness in the air which was quite exhilarating. During the morning the referee, Mr. Newton, of Altrincham, with the Warrington officials and the secretary of the Wigan Club made an inspection of the ground. The War- rington men were all for play, naturally, and, in fact, it was only along touch that the ground was really hard. The Wigan representative was quite against playing, and ultimately the referee decided that the match was" off." This means n dead loss to the club of from £7O to .£lOO, for the gate on Saturday, had the match been played, must - have been of huge dimensions. Luck seems against the Warrington Club just now, and a. turn in the wheel of fortune would be heartily welcomed.</p>
        <p>A number of Northern Union matches came off in spite of the weather, several grounds having been covered against the machinations of "Jack Frost." The "Athletic News" alludes specially to the post- ponement of the Warrington match with the remarks: "The expenditure of a few pounds on straw or other covering would undoubtedly have been a paying business for the club management, as they lost a splendid opportunity of making a big haul out of the Wigan encounter. The mere fact that Wigan and Warrington are racing for pride of place in the League ought to have been sufficient for the officials, and it was ridiculous for them to lament their hard luck, &amp;c. In this variable climate it is unwise to pin faith in Providence as a means of keeping out frost, and those clubs who take ordinary precautions generally find it pays them best in the long run."</p>
        <p>There is a good deal of justice in this criticism though I know the Warrington offic'als will not admit it. They had little warning, it is true, but other clubs managed to get their turf protected in time and con- sequently netted a large and welcome sum in gate money. Warrington are always inclined to trust in Providence to a large extent, and they have been badly frost-bitten before, but they do not seem to learn very easilSr.• The expenditure of covering the ground could not surely, have been considered for a moment, and the only other excuse I have heard is the weak one that the straw damages the turf! It would not do £lOO damage, however, and that is what the frost was allowed to do to the Club. Will there be any precautions in regard to the Bradford match on the 20th? That is the question. lam afraid another freeze-out will create a revolt amongst the Club members.</p>
        <p>Of the matches that were played on Saturday it was a great day for the Yorkshire teams,as Swinton, I Leigh' and St. Helens were treated with scant cere- mony by their hosts, whilst Salford could only make a drawn game with Hull. The one solitary Lanca- shire club that kept their colours flying to the bitter end were the despised Widnes team; who performed a double feat by vanquishing Huddersfield at Fartown and at the same time gave one of their competitors for the last two places a push farther into the Slough of Despond.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows : For Agst.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G.T. P. P. Halifa l4 .11... 1...2...15 21— 93... 8 6 34...24 Bro'ght'n R 13... 9... 2...2 ..15 30-120... 3 4 18...20 Swinton 13... 8... $...21-22 25-119...13 11— 59...18 Salford - 14. 8 2...1...14 15— 73...11 8— 46...18 Bradford 13. 7... 4. 2. 16 19— 89...10 12— 56...16 Leigh l4 7... 5...2...17 15— 79...10 17— 71...16 Runcorn 12 5... 3. 4 .10 16— 68... 8 6 34...14 Oldham l2 7... 5...0...14 17— 79...10 10— 50...14 Hull 14 6... 6...2... 7 19— 71...14 18-- 82...14 Warrington 13... 5... 5...3...10 12— 56...13 14— 68...13 Wigan 12... 5. 5...2.. 9 6 36... 7 10— 44...12 Hmislet 14... 5... 8...1...22 11— 77...19 27-119...11 Batley 12... 5... 7-0...11 12— 58...19 18— 92...10 Widnes 14... 4... 9...1...11 10— 52...14 18— 82... 9 Hull K. R. 14... 4...10...0... 6 13— 51...19 23-107... 8 Huddersfield 13... 3... 9...1... 6, 8— 36...13 13— 65... 7 Brighouse 1113... 3... 9...1... 7 6 32...15 16— 78... 7 St Helens 14... 3...10...1...15 6 48...21 30-132... 7</p>
        <p>Warrington at present rejoiee in the usual point per match characteristic of the Qlub's capabilities. They also occupy the centre of the table, for which position they have apparently taken a several years' lease.</p>
        <p>To-d%Warrington go to Widnes, where they can rely on getting a warm reception. Just now the Chemicals are going strongly, while Warrington's form has deteriorated. The odds are certainly in favour of the home side, but I am not without hope that the "class" on the Warrington side will once more assert itself. A good game is pretty certain, and there will doubtless be a big percentage or War- ringtonians in the perfumery capital.</p>
        <p>Warrington will be represented by : Hallam, ful- back ; Fish, Isherwood, Brooks, and Harris, three quarters; Burgess and • Hockenhall, half-backs' Morrison, Edmondson, Harmer, Meacock, Swift, Naylor, Forster, and Lunt, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual meeting of the P.S.A. was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earle-street. on Sunday last. Mr. Woods presided over a very large attendance. An inter- esting address on "Friendship" was given by Mr. R. King. Several vocal items were contributed by Miss L. Barr. Mr. Morgan contributed recitations, and Miss Williamson accompanied at the piano. "AQUATIC AUTOCRATS AND VAIRIRS." _ . _</p>
        <p>This was the subject of a lecture given in the Viaduct Institute, Earlestown, on Thursday evening, by Mr. Fred Enok, F.L.S., &amp;c. Mr. C. W. Emmett presided,but there was not a large audience, due, probably, to the very cold night. The lecture was illustrated by lantern slides,and was descriptive chiefly of the lives and habits of the various insects which exist upon and in the water. WOUNDING AT NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Assizes on Tuesday, Alma Blanch Kelly, (35), Hazel Grove? Newton' who had been on bail, was indicted with having at Newton-le-Willows, on the 20th 13ptember, feloniously wounded Maria S. Kenny.—Mr. Rigby Swift prosecuted, and stated that on the date named, when Mrs Kenny was in conversation with a neighbour, prisoner struck her with a bottle, inflicting rather serious injury.—The jury found prisoner guilty of unlawful wounding, and she was sentenced to three months' imprisonment with hard labour. • CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, NEWTON. In connection with this church and in aid of the debt Mall remaining on the renovation fund, a "Crown" Sale of Work was held in the Schoolroom on Thursday. The sale was opened in the presence of a very moderate attendance of the public by the Rev. James iillatt who explained the object for which it was being held, and stated that they desired to raise only £5O which would enable them to free themselves from debt in connection with the particular fund men- tioned: The display on the various stalls was only of moderate proportions, the intention being not to sell any article at a higher price than five shillings,hence its designation of "Crown" sale. As the evening drew on there was a fairly large attendance of buyers and the stall-holders did a brisk business. It is hoped that the sum needed will be exceeded. WESLIMAN CHURCH, EARLESTOWN.—SALE OF WORK AND CHRISTMAS TREE.</p>
        <p>A sale of work and Christmas tree was opened on Thursday afternoon in the Lecture Hall of the above church in aid oi the debt which is owing on the renova- tion and enlargement scheme of the Lecture Hall. It is hoped to raise a sum of £.50. The Ladies' Sewing Society have had the arrangements on hand and deserve pedal praise for the excellent manner in which theyr have decorated the stalls and room. There was a fail, attendance and the Rev. C. H. Dove took the chair. Mist% Chandley, the secretary of the Ladies' Sewing Society was announced to open the sale. The Chairman in a few remarks said that they were greatly indebted to the members of the Ladies' Sewing Society who had done all in their power to provide them with increased coma' fort and accommodation. Miss Chandley in declaring the sale open appealed to those present for hearty sup- port in the good work they were doing to enlarge the premises and make them more comfortable. The sale of goods was then proceeded with. During the evening concerts were given by the children trained by Mesdames Dove and Downes. NEWTON CONSERVATIVE CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Friday evening, a smoking concert was held in the Conservative Club, Earlestown when the prizes won in the recent billiard handicap were presented. Mr. J. Robinson presided and was supported by Messrs. C. K. F. Borron, A. C. Leslie, T. Haselden and W. A. Mawdsley. The Chairman said it was gratifying to have such a number of working Conservatives present, as it had been remarked that Conservative working men were vastly disappearing from political life. They had to con- gratulate themselves in having the present Government still in office. The business capacity and foresight of the Government maintained and the prosperity of the country which meant everything to working men. He referred to the Education Bill which, he said, would enably workingmen's children to pass from the elementar schools to the colleges. He trusted the Bill would pass the House or Lords. Mr. Borron said he was pleased to see Mr. Robinson in the chair. He was the mainstay of the Conservative party in Haydock and Golbome. The prizes were distributed to the successful competitors in the billiard handicap and afterwards a good entertain- ment was provided. On the motion of Mr. Borron seconded by Mr. Leslie a vote of thanks was accorded to the artistes who took part in the evening's pro- gramme.—A vote of thanks was also given to the chair- man. ej.a EARLESTOWN WORKMEN'S HOMING SOCIETY. The members of the above society held their annual meeting and dinner on Saturday evening at the Oddfellovvs' Arms, Earlestown. About 60 sat down to „,.. an excellent repast provided by Mr. Lythgoe After dinner the meeting was held, Mr. J. Guy, president of the Liverpool Federation, presiding. He was supported by Mr. J. Jolley. president of the St. Helens and District Federation; Mr. T. Southern president of the arles- town Society, Mr. T. Appleton and other gentlemen connected with homing. The toast of the King having been given, the Secretary (Mr. Appleton) submitted his annual report. The statement showed a total income of £ll4 10s. 7d.. the expenditure was £ll2 7s. id., leaving a balance of £2 3s. 61 The membership totaledsoagainst 52 the previous year. During the past year 2,855 birds had been conveyed in comparison to 2,465 in the previous season. The sum of £65 les. 6d. had been expended in prizes as against £66 12s. In the previ- ous year, That sum did not include the value of special prizes, otherwise there would have been an appreciative increase in the prize money. The principal winners had been J. Lovesay, H. Jackson, and S. Cliff. The Chairman referred to the remarkable per- formance of Mr. J. Kitt's blue checker, which had flown from Nantes and won the first prize in connection with ' their Society, and tenth prize in Liverpool and district open race. Mr. J. Lovesay had also done well in not only winning the first prize for the best bird in his club, but had also carried off a similar prize awarded by the ' St. Helens Federation.—The Chairman proposed the health of the Earlestown Society, and Mr. F. Southern responded.—Mr. Southern proposed a vote of thanks to the visitors, and Messrs. Guy, Jolley, and Edwardson responded. Several musical items were given during the evening. Several prizes were presented to the members for successes in flying matches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>HALTON PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE. The Quality of Runcorn Water.</head>
        <p>HALTON PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE. The Quality of Runcorn Water.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Town Council. The adjourned meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday evening, for the transaction of matters left undisposed of at the meeting of the Commit held on the 2nd of December. The matters in question refer to a motion by Mr. Pemberton respecting the standing orders affecting the .printing of minutes, two motions by Councillor Peacock and one by Councillor Dodds having reference to the trading transactions of the Corporation during the past six years. Manchester Poultry and Pigeon Show. The Fourth Annual Great Show of Prize Poultry, Pigeons, Rabbits, Cats, Cavies and Mice is to be held in St. James's Hall, Oxford-street, Manchester on , Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday next. There are upwards of 4,000 entries including most of the leading exhibits of the kingdom, practically from John O'Groats to Lands End. The prices paid for pigeons and poultry are really astonishing. A Barb Pigeon having been claimed at previous Manchester Shows for £lOO Cash and a Plymouth Rock Cockerel for £5O. Excursion trains are being run from all pa rts to the Show. Buckley-street P.S.A. There was a good attendance at the monthly meet- ing in connection with this society which was held on Sunday in the Methodist Free Church, Buckley-street. I The chair was taken by Mr. J. Owen, and the address was given by Mr. A. E. Morton (St. Helens), and was much appreciated. Mr. W. Shaw read the lesson. Miss Dyson (Liverpool), possesses a beautiful soprano voice and delighted the congregation with her inter- pretations of "The Holy City" and "Angels ever bright and fair." Mr. A. E. Trow was the accom- panist. Miss C. Owen recited "The road to heaven." Lantern Lecture. "Egypt and the Suez Canal route to India and back" was the subject of an interesting and instruc- tive lantern lecture delivered by Mr. John Watson, F.R.A.S., of Warrington at a meeting in connection with the Wycliffe Young People's Society on Monday evening. The Rev John Yon,ge presided and briefly introduced the lecturer. The lecture was illustrated by one hundred beautiful lantern slides and as the audience accompanied the lecturer on their imaginary tour, the chief points at interest were explained by the lecturer who had visited all the places he de- scribed. At the conclusion of an interesting lecture, a vote of thanks to the lecturer was unanimously passed on the proposition of Mr Bindloss, seconded by Mr. Hawthorn. The Sale of Work in Brick-street Mission. About £l2O was the handsome amount realized by this sale of work which was held last Friday and Saturday. Councillor Barton performed the re-opening ceremony on Saturday afternoon. Mr. Roberts pre- sided, and after extending a hearty welcome to Councillor Barton alluded to the need there was for furthering the work of Christ in that neighbourhood. Councillor Barton, in declaring the sale open, said he supposed some thought they at Brick-street were a small, insignificant body, but, as Mr, Roberts said, there was need and room for them. He looked upon the day he entered Brick-street Sunday school as one of the grandest in his life. Since that time, about 40 years ago, he had not lost his connection with it. They had undertaken a great and important work, and he hoped they would increase their usefulness. In con- clusion, he said he hoped the church and the mother church of Friars'-green would go hand in hand together, and that the time would come when they would be as one. (Applause) On the motion of Mr. Warburton, seconded by Mr. H. Jolley, a 'vote of thanks was accorded to Councillor Barton for opening</p>
        <p>the sale. "An Hour with Peter Mackenzie." This was the subject of an interesting lecture delivered under the auspices of Bold-street Wesley Guild on Monday evening by the Rev. D. Rycroft, of Bradford. Mr. R. N. Hartley, presided over a good attendance. The lecturer dealt with Mackenzie's early life, and traced his rise from coal pit to pulpit. Though he received only the most elementary educa- tion, his stirring and eloquent addresses won him great popularity; and his remarkable flow of words, his wit and humour, and his generosity, all served to bring together record congregations. A lover of uprightness and straight dealing, God gave him a great work—and he did it well. Untiring zeal and energy marked his labour throughout his ministry, and his great popularity was further evidenced by the fact that at his death in 189410 left engagements which would have taken three years to fill. Mr. Bean proposed, and Mr. Holland seconded, a cordial vote of thanks to the lecturer, and this was agreed to with acclamation. Concert in the Co-operative Hail, _ .</p>
        <p>A large audience a;sembled in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Monday evening when the annual entertainment in aid of the Sacred Heart schools was held. The major portion of the pro- gramme were contributed by the school children, who had been very carefully trained by the headmistress, Miss Dimsdale, Miss Bonney, and the staff of teachers. They gave a highlyireditable rendition of the two can- tatas 'The Enchanted,Glen"and "Old Friends with New Faces," and.were rewarded with the hearty applause of the audience. The following took part: Mary Quinn, Mary Clarke, James Crannigau, Thomas Boyle, Teresa Crannigan, Edith Lemon, Ralph Riinmer, Joseph Tomkins, Teresa Rogerson, Eliza Corlass, T. Connolly, H. Green, Mr. J. Price, and the, Misses A. Ryan, A. Haywood, If. Reynolds, C. Bolesworth, If. Boyle, A. Crank, C. Walsh, C. Marsh, B. Daley, and A. Farrington' together with a chorus of village girls _ . and fairies. The entertainment concluded with a At the meeting of the above Committee held on Wed- ; musical farce, entitled " Turn him out" or, "The , nesday evening, Mr. T. Wright in the chair with the ,' Lazy Nigger," which created roars of laughter. The ' officials, Messrs. G F. Ashton (clerk), W. j=jiggle (Silt'- I characters were well sustained by Messrs. G. Coggins, veyor, and J. Farrington (inspector), several matters 'G. Newton, S. Ryan, F. V. Eckersley, and W. Fell. , ef importance to the district were discussed. The accompanists, who discharged their duties very THE HALTON SEWAGE SCHEME. I ' The Clerk said that in accordance with instructions, efficiently, were Miss Agnes Richardson and J. he had seen the estate agent of the Bridgewater Trus- l Broadbent. tees and told him that the Council desired to purchases I Warrington Fishdealer Fined at Newton. certain field for a sewage scheme. The agent recom- mended another field near Astmoor farm, but was told ! At Newton Petty Sessions, on Thursday, Isaac by Mr. Ashton that he did not know whether th