        <p>The "A" teams of the two clubs will meet at War- rington, the home side being the same as was selected last week with the exception of Taylor for Lunt in the forwards.</p>
        <p>I On Wednesday Lancashire easily defeated Durham I and Northumberland at Broughton by 10 points to 5 ! points. Fish was unable to play owing to a breach of the Working Clause, and Harris filled the vacancy, but did not play up to his best form. None of the Warrington backs have distinguished themselves of late and a return to their earlier brilliance will be welcomed.</p>
        <p>The General Committee of the Union have made a grant of £5O to the relatives of J. H. Richardson, the Whitehaven half back who received fatal injuries in the recent county match between, Yorkshire and Cum- berland. £lOO has also been granted to the York- shire County Union for the purpose of inaugurating Schools Union Football Competition. This is an ex- periment which will be watched with interest. •</p>
        <p>notes For the match with Cumberland thetheshire Com- mittee have selected the following team to oppose Cumberland at Birkenhead. on the 20th inst. :—Hough- ton (Runcorn), back; Jones and Butterworth (Run- corn), Price (Birkenhead), and Hodgson. (Stockport), three-quarters; Richardson and Jolly (Runcorn), half backs; Walker, Lightfoot, Farmer, Gayter, and Woods (Runcorn), Brown (Birkenhead), and Foster and Simister (Stockport), forwards. Reserves: Jolly (Runcorn), back; Weate (Birkenhead), and Dublin (Runcorn), three-quarters ; Johnson (Stockport), half back; Cook (Birkenhead) and Belington (Stockport), forwards.</p>
        <p>the District Council, THE EXTENSTON OF RtNCORN. The Clerk said he had not had any official intimation re the proposed amalgamation of Runcorn, Halton and Weston. It did not follow that amalgamation would take place because the suggestion came from Runcorn. The Chairman said it was very doubtful whether it would ever come to pass. Runcorn could give them nothing but additional rates. The present skive scheme would not cost them any more than it would cost were Runcorn allowed to treat their sewage. Mr. Manning said they would shortly have, he ex- pected, a formidable deputation waiting on them. Mr. Houghton said they were not going to play their hanky-panky tricks up there. Balton was not to be caught napping. This was all the business of general interest.</p>
