      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Bewsey-road New Organ Musical</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road New Organ Musical</p>
        <p>Services.</p>
        <p>-- 1 There were good congregations at this Church on Sunday when further special services in connection with the opening of the new organ were held. Mr. Hickman presided at the organ, and Mrs Booth was the soloist. ' Mr. Hickman brought out the full powers of the new instrument by a selection of or-an solos from such eminent composers as Bach, Smart: Copocci and Lexuare, which were given with considerable ability. Mrs. Booth sang "My soul thirsteth" (Mendelssohn) during the morning, and excelled herself in a very fine rendering of that difficult solo "I will extol Thee" (Caste) in the evening. In the afternoon a musical service was given by the Bewsey-roadchoir,and-Mr B.Wadsworth,the organist. Councillor 13r0ugh presided and gave a sketch of his ideal congregation. He said he would like to see all present with both hymn books and tune books, also anthems, and taking their own part in the balance of ' voices. Such an arrangement would be very inspiring, and no doubt if he were to preach to such a congrega- tion they would draw all the best out of him. The choir i gave its hearers a musical treat by an exceptionally fine , rendering of the anthem "Jean, priceless treasure" (Dr.• J. B:Roberts). The solos were interpreted by Mrs. Ag Parkinson, and Mr. J. Hannan; the effect beins . heightened by an underpart given by the choir. Mre Hannan gave "The Everlasting Day' in a very abln manner, and Mr Tongue made a variation by a violin solo "Simple Aveu" (Thorne). Mr Wadsworth very efficiently played two organ solos.</p>
