were prohibitive. lion. The use of Quinine is well known, but it has never been satisfactorily combined with these prepare- Mr. FLANNERY said he did not think a public-house Lions, until, after overcoming considerable difficulties, was the proper place for the Council to meet at. As repr ntatives of workingmen's organizations it would the proprietor was able to secure a perfectly uniform not be dignified to meet at a public-house. He pro- pre parati on, combining all the essential properties ofthe posed that the Council should continue to hold meetings above plants in their greatest purity and conceiatrat ion fit Cairo-street school until a more suitable place was It is now established as a family medicine, and is in- found. creasing in popular favour the more it is known . Mr. KENDRICK said the Liverpool Trades Council, and tested. Gwilym Evans, Quinine Bitters is a tonic which had something like 350 delegates, was not "Pick-me-up, Scientifically mixed in happy propor- tions. ashamed to meet at a public-house. There was nothing degrading or elevating about it and the Council could Sold everywhere in bottles 28. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each Should any difficulty be experienced in procuring meet at a public-house without "demoralizing them- Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, it will be forwarded selves with drink." N carriage free, for the above prices, by the sole proprie- Mr. HAxtimgrr seconded Mr. FLANERY'S motion. On the motion of Mr. BLEASE seconded by Mr. tors The Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, BUCKLEY it was decided to adjourn the matter until Limited Llanelly, South Wales. We particularly caution the next meeting, and Messrs. Blease, Hamblett, Ken- our readers against imitators, and impress upon them Ken- the importance of always seeing that the name drick, Flannery and Stott were appointed as a cons- ; Gwilym Evans "is on the Label, Stamp, and Bottle, mittee. A number of places were suggested for the com- without which none is genuine. mittee's consideration including the Police Buildings. PROPOSED TRADES HALL. Referring to the question of a Trades Hall for War- .rington, the SECRETARY said he had written to a large number of secretaries asking for information with regard to the working of halls in other towns but had no particulars, as yet, to offer. They would first want to see which societies would be prepared to take up shares in the society which would have to be formed. They would have to be a registered Land and Building Society in which individual trade unionists or societies could take up shares. If the hall was worked on sound lines he considered it would be a good investment. It would not, however, be much use going on with the scheme without a guarantee that it would be taken up, in a general way by the various societies. If the socie- ties entered into the proposal with enthusiasm he thought it would be a success. It was agreed that the matter be placed in the hands of the Executive Committee with a view to drawing up a scheme.</p>
        <p>- -------- PROPOSED LABOUR BUREAU. The SECRETARY gave notice that at the next meeting he would move that the Town Council be requested to take into consideration the formation of a labour bureau. THE MUNICIPAL BOWLING GREEN PROPOSAL. "THAT BOG THE GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE." The CHAIRMAN said that some time ago they lost sight of a resolution with regard to municipal bowling greens in that bog the General Purposes Committee. They wanted to be getting it out of that bog, and he would give notice of motion with regard to the matter for the next meeting. He had heard that apiece of land which was public property was being used semi- privately by the fair sex connected with the aldermen, councillors, and officials of the Corporation, for the pur- pose of a tennis-ground. If that were sot he wanted to know why they could not have a municlval bowling green for the working men who owned that piece of land and had paid for it. A discussion with reference to the unemployed ques- tion is reported in another column.</p>
