      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>AN ACTOR'S EXPERIENCE.</head>
        <p>AN ACTOR'S EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>IThe theatrical profession have ever been appreciative users of Dodd's Kidney Pills, and find them excellent for recuperative purposes. The constant change,variety, and excitement of the profession is a heavy drain onthe vitality of its members, and they above all need a remedy for Rheumatism, Sciatica, and other Uric Acid troubles; Nervousness, Depression, Sleeplessness, and Loss of Memory, which are mostly due to the kidneys. That Dodd's Kidney Pills are an effective remedy for such ills in all manner of people is shown by Mr. H. A. Benson. Mr. Benson, who sends us his photograph, is a bright, merry-faced leading comedian, well known to northern audiences. He wrote to us on Nov. 10th from the Grand Theatre, Chorley, relating his experiences of Dodd's Kidney Pills. He says: "As a member of the theatrical profession I can testify to the benefits derived from a course of Dodd's Kidney Pills. Of all men, it is necessary for the actor to preserve his memory and keep a clear head. I have found during a long experience that whenever I feel nervous or out of sorts a few Dodd's Kidney Pills are sufficient to put me in form again; the result being that 1 feel quite a different man. I heartily recommend them to all who suffer from diseases arising from defec- tive kidneys." Mr. H. Benson's experience is the experience of thousands—men and women of every class and of every profession. Dodd's Kidney Pill will cure the worst kidneys, and with the kidneys well the functional derangements quickly disappear and the bodily health is restored. Dodd's Kidney Pills are 2/9 per box, of all Chemists, or post free on receipt of price by the Dodds Medicine Co.. 23, Farringdon Avenue, London, E.C. Remember the name, D-o-d-d-'s.</p>
        <p>A Boy's Ingenious Trick. Christopher Beelan, of Market-street, Widnes, a boy who is employed at Messrs. Pilkington's Sheet Glass Works, was charged, at St. Helens, on Monday, with stealing 12s. from Messrs. Pilkington's • Foy- office. Constable Winuard said that on Saturday afternoon he received information that ns., the wages of A boy named Hussey, was missing. He searched the prisoner and another boy, but found only .I.s. more on the prisoner than his wages. He said this was his own. Later witness went to Widnes and ar- rested the prisoner. In answer to the charge the boy said, "I saw the box of money lying there in the pay- office window, and nobody would take it, and I took it, and when I got it there was 12s. in it. I had the half-sovereign in my mouth when you searched me. I gave mv mother 13s. 9d. of my wages. Chief Con- stable Wood said the boy had no father, and his mother was over six.tv years of age. He was the only one working out of a-family of ,five. He had an elder brother who was unable to work at all owing to ill- health. The Chairman (Mr. B. B. Glover) said the magistrates were very sorry to see the lad in that position, but for the sake of his family they hoped he would not do anything of the sort again. He was bound over under the First Offenders Act.</p>
        <p>An explosion occurred on Tuesday in the South , Wilkesbarre Mine, in Pennsylvania, and four men were killed and ten injured. The mine belongs to the Leh- I igh and Wilkesbarre Coal Company. The mine is one of the S ost gaseous in the region, and extra precautions are always taken by the men working there. The explosion is said to have been caused by a miner care- lessly dropping a stick of dynamite, which exploded, causing the gas to ignite.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>Minor Cases. Warrington Borough Court. MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair), Alder- man Smethurst, and Messrs. Birchall, Hayward, Wright and Thornton.</p>
        <p>WINDOW Smssimva.—James and Bridget Riley, strangers to the town, were charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Cloth Hall yard on Saturday even ng. The woman was further charged with break- ing three panes of glass at the house of Thomas Daley, doing damage to the amount of 1/6.—lt appears that P.C. Gilchrist found the prisoners in Cloth Hall yard under the influence of drink and creating a disturb- ance. The woman had broken three panes of glass at Mr. Daley's house, with a brick. Prisoners were taken into custody and were very disorderly on the way to the Police Station.—Prisoners were each fined 5/- and costs and the woman was ordered to pay the amount of the damage. LIVED NowuartE.—James Finnerty, 31, Tilley-street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Orford-street at 1.10 on Sunday morning.—P.C. Redding- ton said he found the prisoner lying helplessly drunk on the footpoth. He asked hint where he lived and he replied "I live nowhere." Prisoner then became very disorderly, and used very bad language. He had two contused wounds at the back of the head. Witness bandaged them and took prisoner to the Police Station and he was afterwards removed to the Infirmary in a cab.—Fined ss. and costs.</p>
        <p>BROKE 40 POUN-D-S-iTS-TE. AD OF 27c wT.—Patrick Legh, an inmate of the Workhouse, was charged with refusing to perform the task allotted to him.—John Constable, porter, stated that on Wednesday, Thursday, Friday and Saturday, the prisoner was set to break nine cwt. of stones, tut in the three and a half days he only broke 40 pounds instead of 27 cwt.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for 21 days. DISORDER IN SANKEY-STREET.—WaIter Clough, 61, Back Aikin-street, was summoned for committing a breach of the peace by disorderly behaviour in Sankey- street on the 23rd November. P.C. Cooper proved the case, and said the defendant was under the influence of drink and used very bad language. Defendant was bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs (75.) ORSC&amp;NE LANGUAGE.—Three days imprisonment was the sentence imposed upon a youth named Thomas Broderick, of Brown's-yard, for making use of obscene language in Winwiek-street on November 9th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>SCHOOL TEACHER FINED FOR ASSAULT.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL TEACHER FINED FOR ASSAULT.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, before Messrs. A. Thomas (in the chair) and W. Timmins, a school teacher named John Henry Broadbent, Church- street, was summoned for having assaulted Harold Ennion, a boy under the age of 14 years at Runcorn on the Ist December. Mr. Pletcher, Northwich, appeared to prosecute on behalf of Superintendent Ennion, the boy's father and Mr. Linaker appeared for the defence. The evidence showed that on Monday last Harold Ennion was having a drawing lesson together with a boy named Banner at the Trinity Schools. Banner for committing a breach of discipline was caned by Broad- bent, the master, in front of the class. Ennion on see- ing his companion caned shouted out "Kick him George." After Banner had received his punishment the teacher called Ennion out and told the boy to hold out his hand. Ennion refused, seised the cane, broke it and kicked the teacher's shine. Mr. Broadbent there- upon sent for another cane, struck the boy across the shoulders and afterwards gave him six strokes on the hand.</p>
        <p>The prosecution alleged that the punishment was far too excessive, that the held teacher, Mr. Annett had been informed of the boy's delicate state of health, and that the lad had heen made dangerously ill in conse- quence. The medical evidence showed that the boy's back was severely bruised and the lingers were also bruised the skin being broken. In Dr. MacDougalls opinion the boy had received too severe a castigation. Mr. Annett, head master at Trinity Schools, said that Mr. Broadbent, senior certificated assistant teacher had full power to chastise pupils. For the defence it was further urged that the boy had not received excessive punishment in as much as the medical evidence di,: not show that the boy was likely to suffer harm. After a short consultation the bench decided that the punishment was excessive and the defendant would be fined RI and costs (R 2 (is.) or in default a month's im- prisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Warrington Working Men's Flying Club.</head>
        <p>Warrington Working Men's Flying Club.</p>
        <p>The annual dinner and -distribution of prizes in con- nection with the above, t)ok place on December 4th at the Blue Bell Inn, about 40 sitting down to an ex- cellent dinner provided by Host C.3oper. After the "'loth had been removed, the toast to the king was proposed by the chairman, Mr. Pagant, of Ainsdale Mr. W. Hands then sang a comic scng. The secre- tary was called upon to read his report, which showed a total income of 00 128 10141 ; expenditure, £5.2 Is 6d ; and a balance. in hand of 14s 41d; leav- ing .E 37 17s to be paid in prize money. The prizes were then distributed by Mr Pagant. During the evening Mr. Mead gave two excellent cornet solos. Mr. Haslam gave a splendid rendition of "Mona," Mr. Cruikshank ably presiding at the piano. The toast of "The Host and Hostess' brought an enjoyable even- ingto a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>riTE EXAMINAI FRIDAY 1)E(! BER 12 102 SAMPLES OF LIBERTY.</head>
        <p>riTE EXAMINAI FRIDAY 1)E(! BER 12 102 SAMPLES OF LIBERTY.</p>
        <p>in Chicago a citizen was give an taste of thenight- TRAGIC DEATH OF COLONEL stick, dragged insensible to the nearest patrol box, the hurry-up waggon sent for, and the man thrown into a cell. On the morrow the charge brought LAMB. against him by the bold office! was, "He guy me de laffi—see ? an' I reached for him wid my locus He's English, added the .officer, 1 way. of apology. • ' * SUICIDE ON AN ATLANTIC LINER. "Prisoner discharged,' said the judge. Yet there was no reparation for the indignity. So the , ex-prisoner followed the officer out into the hallway I A Notable Marksman. and yerked him here behind the ear, sending the bluecoat yammering among the Cook County spittoons. It was a foolish move, for the man then got more 'locust' than ever. Chicago policemen not only capture their man, but often punish him then and there. In Delaware they use the cat-o'-nine tails, in Cook County a club suffices. The offender in this instance being well drubbed and clubbed, was brought back into court and given ninety days for assaulting an officer in uniform. By paying a lawyer twenty-five dollars the man got out on habeas corpus proceedings, after serving five' days on the stone pile. But he had better keep away from Driscoll of the Sixth Precinct. 'Belied, iif I ver see ye on me beat, I'll not let you off so slay' were the parting words of the officer." November 4th, 1902. 3118, Guerrers-street, San Francisco. PICTURES BY TELEGRAPH.</p>
        <p>[BY MR. H. WIIITHAM.]</p>
        <p>IWhen we speak of advancing civilization we ought to beak in mind the various qualifications that sur- round the idea. As a matter of fact it is only a little way back from civilization to savagery. A great many fearful crimes have been committed in the name of , Liberty, and some of us remember the long and heroic I struggle made by Mr. Bradlaugh for his seat in Par- liament. The present writer well remembers the fruitless qfforts he made to obtain for Mr. Bradlaugh if hearing in the Co-operative Hall,and how he was met by the senseless phrase that it was ' likense and not liberty' that he was advocatin. Times have changed, and a Unitarian would hardly take the same stand now. II refer to this matter because what follows might lead a few readers to fancy that in giving samples of , American liberty I was under the impression that I there were no grievances to be remedied, and no I wrongs to be righted in my native land. I am far I from thinking so. Liberty is something to fight and I struggle for; but it is something more than that, the word itself stands for an educational principle not to I be gained by any one who is burdened by inherited or acquired prejudice. In America, one might almost say from the Atlantic to the Pacific Ocean, outrages , against liberty, and criminal acts of violence are of almost daily occurrence. Law and order are openly I defied, and it is almost impossible to bring the offen- ders to justice. It is not only a pitiable fact that the citizen is not law abiding, but those who are placed in power, and are given authority, are too often the greatest of criminals. This may be partly due, and I think is largely, due to the manner in which I we give men who have barely had time to take out their citizenship papers, and certainly have not had time to learn the true inwardness of liberty, authority</p>
        <p>! Profound regret has been created in local military circles by the sensational su cide of Colonel T. Lamb, the well-known rifle shot, late of the South Lancashire Regiment. Colonel Lamb went to America early in the year with a rifle team in order to enter the peat inter- national shoot ng match which was held m Canada a couple of months ago. The team came home, but the colonel, for some unknown reason, remained behind,and did not start for England until a fortn ghtago,when the Cunard liner Etruria left New York for Liverpool. During the Voyage there was nothing to show that he 'contemplated the shocking deed which he committed on Sunday, being genial and pleasant in his general demeanour. A middle-aged man, of tall, soldierly ap- pearance, Colonel Lamb was always welcome in com- pany, and proved an agreeable companion. Something, however, seems to have preyed upon his mind, for early on Sunday morning, as Liverpool was being sighted, he fastened the door of his compartment in the saloon. One of the stewards happened to be passing the spot about three o'clock in the morning when he heard the eport of a gun, and on going towards the quarter whence he supposed the sound had proceeded, found bloodstains on the window of Colonel Lamb's berth. On trying the poor he found it locked from the inside. He at once tought the ship's doctor, who on reaching the spot had an entrance forced, when the colonel was found dead on he floor with part of his head blown away. His Lee- Mstford rifle lay across his body. The place was bespattered with blood. Beside the deceased officer were several letters in his handwriting, one adeddress to the captain of the vessel apologising for the incon- venience the dreadful act would cause him, another giving reasons for the crime, and others intended for relatives.</p>
        <p>PicTußEs are now sent by telegraph, by means of an American invention. The original picture is impressed on a roller covered by a fine wire screen, which revolves in contact with a needle point, very much like the roller of a phonograph. The needle is connected with the telegraph line, and at the other end, a similar needle reproduces by its pressure a picture like the one trans- mitted. The effect is shown in the following illustra- tion.</p>
        <p>over others. I A ease occurred recently in this city of a young and most respectable girl protesting against the way in !which four policemen handled a helpless drunkard whose head had been clubbed by one of their number • until it bled. The girl called out, "Oh you mur- derers!" I Directly she uttered these words she was grabbed bythe policeman that had done the clubbing, and 'bundled into the patrol wagon that was in waiting at I the curb. The sleeve of the girl's dress was torn by the grip of the policeman. The crowd that had gathered hissed and muttered as the patrol wagon was driven swiftly away. The girl in grief and shame at the indignity that had been done her, wept as she Protested against her arrest. Before the I patrol wagon had gone more than half a block the policeman thought better of his bargain, stopped the I vehicle and released the sobbing girl. There was no Iredress for this outrage, she was simply released, and , there was an end to the episode. What we lack, • seems to be a strong public opinion. Now let us turn to the people themselves. In the matter of the numerous lynchings that occur. In these almost unspeakable atrocities there does seem to be a ; public opinion but it is an opinion run-a-muck in-as- much as it takes the law into its own hands instead of seeing to it that the law shall be effective and proving their ability to stand by it. The following occurred in Forest City, Arkansow : "Charles Young, a negro charged with murdering Mrs. Edward Lewis, a white I woman, was burned to-night by a mob of citizens of I this county. Sheriff W. E. Williams used every effort I against this extreme measure. He had telephoned to Judge Hutton Of this district, who had promised to come to-morrow and give immediate trial for the nee) and the leaders of the mob had given the Sheriff positive pledges that they would await the trial and take no violent measures. About 8.30 o'clock to-night a mob marched to the jail-and demanded the keys. Deputy Sheriff Murphy undertook to - Le with Sheriff Williams, whereupon members of the mob forcibly took the cell keys from Murphy, and breaking the jail door with sledge hammers, dragged the prisoner from his cell. Sheriff Williams had arrived in the meantime, his pro- tests not being heeded. The mob took the negro to a point about half a mile east of town, bound him, piled wood around him and set fire to it. The negro begged piteously for his life, but the mob turned deaf ears. ln a short time the flames leaped up and he expired in the presence of the several hundred men composing the mob. After Young had been put to death, the mob started in quest of another negro, alleged to have been implicated in the killing of Mrs. Lewis, ' These cases could be multiplied to almost any extent, but what is the use? The only excuse I have for referring to them at all is because the press is so constant in its , endeavours to make the world believe that only in' America is true liberty to be found. The bad feature of this self-laudation is that it lulls the people into a false and hateful conceit. They never weary of hear- ing the eagle cry out its shibboleth, and therefore fail to perceive the treacherous forces at work under- mining what little of liberty they may have. And yet there are thinkers who are fully alive to the dangers surrounding the Republic. There are men who write in strong and manly words of warning, the caution needed by our men and women of the New World. Men who realise as fully as man can some- thing of what this Republic means to the world of the future, such a one is Elbert Hubbard, editor of the "Philistine," one of the best of the bibelot series issued in America, and from which I give the follow- ing rather long quotation: "It is only a little way back from civilization to savagery. However, we are making headway. The last decade has seen changes that mark epochs in the onward march. To mention but two items, we might name the almost complete change of our definition as to what constitutes "Christianity," and in material things the use of electricity has worked such a revolution as even Jules Vern e never conjured forth. But we have quite a way to go yet. We Americans are somewhat given to car - ing our country "The Land of the Free"as if there were no other. But if we want an example of the best government we will have to go to New Zealand or Switzerland. In point of maximum literacy and minimum poverty, the Swiss excel any country in the world. The Swiss have no navy and no standing army. But the individual in England to-day really has greater freedom of speech and action, more privi- leges, than the individual in the United States. In every large city of America there is an extent of petty officialism and dictation that the English people would not for a day endure. Our policemen, following their Donnybrook proclivities, are all armed with clubs, and allowiug pre-natal influences to lead, they often unlimber the motto "Wherever yOU See a head hit it." on slight excuse., In Central Park, New York for instance, the citizen who "talks back" is speedily clubbed into silence,—but try that thing in Hyde Park, London, and see wh,at would follow! But thank heaven, we are working out our salvation all the time,—things are getting better and it is to the "dissatisfied" that we owe all progress. Were we satisfied, we would stand still, and to stand still is to retreat. In most American cities the people exist for the police, in London the police are the servants of the people. It is absolutely astounding what we have to put up , with in the way of undue officialism in this country. The evil has been carried to such a head as to have ; evoked a striking decision from Judge Gaynor of New York, one of the bravest and most upright in the American judiciary. If you are arrested and ; clubbed without warrant, you may get a little corn- fort by reading the words of Judge Gaynor, which ; for this purpose may be conveniently pasted in your , bat :</p>
        <p>HOW IT WAS DONE. There was no difficulty in ascertaining how the colonel had taken his life. Attached to the trigger of the rifle was a piece of string, the other end of which was tied to his right foot. It was obvious from this, and from the position of the weapon as it lay on the body, that the deceased soldier had placed the muzzle of the gun in his mouth, and pulled the trigger bya movement of the foot. From various articles found in his possession and in the berth it would appear that • Colonel Lamb had spent a considerable portion of the night in writing letters to friends and relatives, a num- ber of such communications being addressed and stamped ready for the post. The statement explaining the motive of the deed was pinned to the wall of his</p>
        <p>sleeping berth.</p>
        <p>Marriage of Miss Jeffery. An interesting wedding took place, at Wycliffe Church, Bewsey-street, at noon on Wtinesday, the I contracting parties being Mr. T. H. Pretty, manager of the forwarding department of Messrs Peter Walker and Sons Ltd., Warrington and Liverpcol, and Miss Edith Mabel, youngest daughter of Mr. William Jeffery, retired manager of Messrs. Rylands, Bros. Limited. The bride has been for a number of years a teacher at Wycliffe Day Schools and she was the recipient of a silver cake-basket from the teachers and scholars as a wedding present. A large congrega- tion witnessed the marriage ceremony, a number of scholars from the adjoining schools taking great interest in the proceedings. During the assembling of the guests Mr, W. H. Payton, Mus Bac played selections on the organ, including "Cavatina" '(Raft) and "My heart ever Faithful" ( Bach). IThe bride, who was given away by her • father, looked exceedingly handsome and was attired, in a I costume of white glace silk with trimmings of chiffon, I lace, and orange blossom. She also wore a chiffon hat with ostrich plumes and carried a shower bouquet I composed chiefly of white chrysanthemums, lilies and camelias. There were three bridesmaids in attend- ance, namely, Miss Elsie Winskill (niece of the bride), Miss Mabel Payton and Miss Blanche Payton. Miss Winskill was attired in white silk, with lace inser- tion, and hat to match and wore a gold chain and pendant set with turquoises, the gift of the bride- groom. The Misses Payton wore dresses of lavender cashmere with trimmings of cream lace and hats to match. Their ornaments were gold curb bracelets, also the gifts of the bridegroom. Each bridesmaid carried a beautiful bouquet in which chrysanthe- mums predominated. 'lle duties of best man were faithfully discharged by Mr. T. B. Partington with Mr. A. Thompson as groomsman. The officia- ting minister was the Rev. John Yonge. At the con- clusion of the ceremony, Mr. Payton played Mendelssohn's "Wedding March," A reception was subsequently held in the Memorial Building, at which about fifty invited guests were present. The cus- tomary toasts were' proposed and duly honoured, and a few hours were pleasantly whiled away in a sociable I manner. The catering was in the capable hands of Mr. John Atkinson. Later in the day the happy couple left for London with the well wishes of their many friends. They were the recipients of numerous , clioice,and costly Wedding presents.</p>
        <p>passing in excellence with the rifle, ilonel Lamb possessed more than ordinary skill with other military weapons</p>
        <p>A singular incident (says the lobby correspondent of the" Times ") occurred in connection with the division upon the Uganda resolution on Tuesday night. When, the question was put from the chair in the House o I Commons messengers were, in accordance with the usual practice, despatched by the Whips in hot haste to apprise such members as might be listening to the debate in the Lords, of the necessity for the immediate return to the Lower Chamber to record their votes. It so happened, however, that a division was in progress in the Upper House at the same moment, which meant that the Prime Minister, Mr. Wyndham, Mr. Asquithe and several other Privy Councillors standing near the Throne were unable to make their escape from the pr- cints of the House of Lords until too late to take prt in the Commons division.</p>
        <p>Any officer who, instead of being in the perform- ance of his duty, is in fact committing a wrongful trespass upon an individual, need not be submitted to, but may on the contrary be lawfully resisted,the same as though he were a private person. His official position does not license him to interfere with and arrest people at his pleasure. On the con- trary, unless the individual has committed a criminal offence, government has no right to arrest him with- 'out a warrant, whether through its police or peace officers, or through any agency or power whatever This is what free government means, A short time ago the newspapers were telling how</p>
        <p>Irated the evidence of Chamberlain as regards the finding of the body. He added that he found the note produced pinned to the wall of the room with the blade of a pen- knife. He also found the six letters produced,addressed to various people and one to the captain. In his opinion death was due to a gunshot wound in the head. Stephen Jones, police-constable, (gave evidence as regards the removal of the body to Prince's Dock Mortu- ary. The note and letters were handed to him by Mr. Johnston. The rifle had one discharged and three undis- charged chambers. i " NO OBJECT IN LIFE," I Witness Gill (recalled) said that the documents , handed to him were in the deceased's handwriting. I The Coroner then read some letters. The first was to , the following effect :--" I consider that the Palma , Trophy team was perfectly fairly drawn,and Freemantle a model captain, but I had no idea that the team was going to be chosen by the scores made in the practices. There is no doubt that I went to Canada under false pretences, and had to live on other people's money. I could not dream of using the return half of the Beaver line ticket. I see no object in life. I care for no one, since no one cares for me. I swear before God now in the hour of death that I commit suicide purely and simply because I see no object in living any longer. If I were a married man with children I would not do this. Kissock and Elmer say that the pain in my head is from the ship rolling. I hope soon to stop all pain. I have had a bottle of whisky a day." The Coroner said that with regard to the two names mentioned by the deceased in his 'letter, they were fellow-passengers of the deceased, and he was sorry that they were not present, because they would have given them some more information as to the state of the deceased's mind. Perhaps they did not think they were required, otherwise they would have stopped. It looked as though deceased had been talking to them about pains in his head.</p>
        <p>The next letter was addressed to the captain of the vessel, and read as follows :—" Sir,—l am extremely sorry for what lam going to do on your vessel, and especially for the mess I may make of my stateroom. I would have dropped over the side instead, only I was afraid you might pick me up before I drowned. I must earnestly request you will bury, my remains at sea. Gill, your fireman, can identify me, and that is all I require. My belongings should be sent to 145, War- wick-street, London. There is some money in my bag to pay for the damage I have done to the cabin.—Yours Thomas Lamb."</p>
        <p>Proceeding, the Coroner said in addition to these two letters, six other letters had been found, and he would have considered it his duty to open them and read their contents if he thought they had anything to do with the cause of death. They were, however, addressed to his own people and other friends, and .might contain some- thing which had no bearing on the case at all. He (the coroner) regretted very mnch that there was no member of the family present, because they might have given them some further information. The reason was that General Lamb was an elderly gentleman and not able to travel. It was clear, however, that deceased suffered from pain in his head, and it might be that he had been disappointed in some way about the rifle team. The jury found a verdict of i` Suicide during tem- porary insanity." Removal of the Remains.</p>
