        <p>vastly superior to ordinary cough miiturea or any of the emulsions, Thousands of sufferers in Great Britain have been cured after having been turned out of hospi- tals. It has an enormous sale because of its superior virtue in all cases of Chronic coughs, bronchitis,asthma, catarrh, influenza, and especially children's coughs. Ask for VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE, is lid and 2s 9d at chemists and medicine vendors everywhere. A valu- able book on chest diseases accompanies each bottle.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's Men's Bible Class. Complete success attended the third annual social promoted by the members of this Bible Class which' took place in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Tuesday evening. Praise is due to Mr. Robinson, Mr. Broadhurst, and the members of the committee, for the excellent arrangements which had been made. ; The class has now a membership' 0f.147, and as many I of the members were accompanied by friends, the ; attendance at the social numbered about 200. Mr. I A. C. Binks presided and amongst those present were: The vicar (the Rev. W. Bracecamp), and Mrs. Bracecamp. the Rev. Clement J. Edwards ! ' (curate), the lay reader (Mr. John Coxon), Mr. R. J. ' Chorley (vicar's warden), and Mr. J. H. Smethurst (people's warden). Dancing was indulged in with I zest, and during the intervals there were songs &amp;c. Miss Armstrong very sweetly sang "The flight of ages" and "Robin Adair, and was heartily. applauded. Mr. Lightwood creditably rendered "The ! owl," and Mr Hindley gave a spirited interpretation of a patriotic song. A capital recitation by the Rev. C. J. Edwards was well received, and humorous' songs by Mr Coxon created considerable amusement I Selections on the phonograph by Mr C. Chorley added ; additional interest to the proceedings. There was I also two interesting competition,sone for ladies and one for gentlemen, A. very enjoyable function was I brought to a close about midnight.</p>
