      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Inquest—Pathetic Letters.</head>
        <p>The Inquest—Pathetic Letters.</p>
        <p>The Liverpool City Coroner (Mr. T. E. Sampson), heldan inquiry touching the death of Colonel Lamb, on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. Paxton appeared for the relat ves of the deceased gentleman, and Mr. Furness (Messrs. Hill, Dickinson and Co.) for the Cunard Company. The first witness was. • Thomas Gill. marine fireman. of Bird-street, Liver- pool. He deposed that from 1881 to 1888 he was a soldier in the Ist Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment, and the deceased at that t me was captain of the "G " (witness's) Company. Witness under- stood that the deceased was out in the South African war. Witness had left. the Army. On Friday, the 27th of last month, witness was working on board the Etruria, that being the day before she sailed from New York. Deceased was a passenger on I board, and witness spoke to him. Deceased told him he had lost an eye at Spion Rop. He also said he was going over by the Etruria, and would see witness later. Witness saw him once again, but did not speak to him. Deceased told him that he was a retired colonel. He seemed to be in as good health as he was years ago when witness acted as his servant. I Richard Chamberlain stated that he was a night steward on board the Etruria, and was on the vessel during its last voyage from New York to Liverpool. About three o'clock on Sunday morning last when the vessel was in the Crosby Channel he heard a sound which resembled the report of a rifle shot. He tried to locate the sound, and in doing so he found what he thought to be bloodstains inside the window of No. 90 I state-room, which was occupied by the deceased. Wit- ness knocked at the door, but got no response. The door was bolted on the inside. He then called the ship's doctor, who had the door forced open. Witness then found the deceased in a sitting position against the door, apparently having shot himself through the head with a rifle (produced). The weapon was lying with the nozzle on his chest, and' directed towards his head. There was a piece of string attached to the trigger, and from the position of the deceased he could have put i his foot n the lbop of the string and pulled the trigger in that way. The doctor found a note and some letters in the room. The man was quite dead. Deceased had never complained to witness about anything during the voyage, neither had he heard him complain to anybody. The voyage was a rough one, and the vessel rolled a great deal. He saw deceased very often, and noticed nothing peculiar about him. Thomas Duncan, bedroom steward on board the Etruria, said he lakt saw deceased alive at half-past eleven on Saturday n ght. He then appeared to be in very good spirits. Witness spoke to him about getting I his baggage out, but deceased told him not to remove the gun-case, as it was not ready. His suspicions were not aroused in any way by the deceased's demeanour. •William Thomas, bar attendant of the vessel, was on duty during the voyage,which lasted eight days. During that time deceased bought from witness six bottles of whisky. That was not an extraordinary quantity. He got the last bottle on the Friday, and the last part of that had not been consumed. On Thursday witness had a little talk with the deceased, who told him that he was not drinking all the whisky, as his friends had it as I well. Witness never saw him drunk; he was always sober when he came to witness- He always appeared to be rational. He did not hear him complain about his eye, or of pains in his head. Benjamin Johnston, surgeon on the Etruria, corrobo-</p>
      </div>
