      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>WYCLIFFE TEMPERANCE SOCIETY. Rev. J. F. Matthews and the Green Dragon Licence.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE TEMPERANCE SOCIETY. Rev. J. F. Matthews and the Green Dragon Licence.</p>
        <p>A public meeting under the auspices of the Wycliffe Temperance Society was held in the Memorial Building on Wednesday evening. Mr. S. B. Stringer presided, •,' and he was accompanied on the platform by the Rev. John Yonge, Rev. J. F. Matthews and Mrs. Simpson. 1 The CHAIRMAN spoke a few encouraging words to ;g1 temperance workers generally and pointed out that there was no reason to be disheartened with the results of their labours. Mrs. SIMPSON, in the course of an instructive address said there was a great deal of talk at the present time about national efficiency. Those who were arrayed on the side of temperance must go in for temper- ance efficiency. (Hear, hear.) There was a great deal • , of temperance work to be done inall walks of life, and if each would take a part the cause would flourish much • more than it did, She was glad that their local magis- trates had risen to the occasion with reference to licensing applications. The magistrates had full power to use their own discretion as to how many licenses should be granted in it certain district, and they ought to be good judges as to whether the drink traffic was a benefit or a curse to a community. By exercising their authority over the licensed houses, the magistrates could do much for the sobriety of the town. Instances of the terrible evils attending • the drink traffic were given by the speaker, and she asked whether they were trying to reclaim any of the victims. It was I very difficult to reclaim a drunken man or woman but they must not despair. Let them do what they could to uphold and help those people who were doing this work, and what was more important still, let them train , 1 the young in the paths of temperance and remove OW " : temptations which beset them. (Applause.) j The Rev. J. F. 'MATTHEWS referred to the practice of , many people during the festive season of offering wine lat children's parties. He advised the young people I present to refuse to take it and urged the I adults to refrain from offering such A tempta- tion to boys and .girls. Proceeding to refer to ; the inducements to drink which were offered to young people when they went out into the world to earn a , livelihood, the speaker asked whether it was not possible ; for parents and even employers of lour to exercise a • little wise care in the guardianshlTl of young people when theycommenced work. He knew of many careers ' which had been blighted just for the want of a little care on entering a business career. The speaker strongly condemned the practice of what was known as "pay- ing your footing" in many workshops. Customs, he said, like prejudices, died hard; but it was I time that employers and labour and workingmen I stopped this practice. Continuing he said 'he had noticed with particular pleasure the action of Ithe magistrates of Warrington in refusing to grant the transfer of the Green Dragon licence in Bridge-street to Lovely-lane. He was sure all who were capable of ; appreciating the meaning of that -action would attach it ~.; Igreat deal of importance to it. (Hear, hear.) It meant. _iv, . this that the Warrington public, led by the Warrington magistiates, were beginning to open their eyes to this fact that the brewing companies were only too eager to I give up licences which in many instances were of little -_ I value to them, in order to get into new and populous 1 districts where there were no public-houses. By sup- " I ' porting the magistrates in their action, as the public had done on this occasion, they would make it more and 1 more possible for the magistrates to act as they had so , nobly done recently, and he ventured to think that in course of time they would have a perceptible change in the drinking habits of the people of Warrington Redid ' not despair of witnessing such an improvement. The ,1 people needed educating in the scientific aspect of the 1 temperance question as well as the moral and religious ' aspects, and he believed they were moving in that direc- tion. (Hear' herar.) I A musical programme was submitted. Solos were r sung by Miss A. Griffiths and Mr. F. Berry, and a trio I was contributed by the Brothers Galloway. Miss 1 Robinson accompanied on the piano. Mr. G. Whitefield I recited and his son gave selections on the auto-harp. : Mr:Hassall was present with his gramophone and gave several selections i A vote of thanks to those who had taken part was I passed on the proposition of the Rev. JO FIN YONG E.</p>
        <p>. A PROSPEROUS ComPANY.—The report of the Amal I gamated Press (Harmsworth Brothers) for the year ended October 31, shows that the profits—after writing ; .4 off £25,000 for depreciation and £20,840 against copy.- 1 1 , right account—amount to £249,172. An interim dim I Mrs. Cox, 40, St. Mary's-rziad, Southampt on, writes ..' r dead on the ordinary shares at the rate of 25 per cent. I —"I have not drunk any tea for three years last July, ' per annum has been paid, and the directors now recom- mend lam a total abstainer, so I feel I am doing my mend a further dividend and bonus, making the distri- bution for the year 40 per cent. The gross profits duty in writing. I find ,Vi-Cocoa more strengthening amounted to the large sum of £708,170, of which paper ' and nourishing than any other kind of Cleo,, anfeel • d I . and printing absorbed £277,223, editorial salaries and literary expenses £77,184 and advertising £33,204. able to do any amount of work. I have a family, of five WOODEN LEGS ABLAZE.—An old watchman, Leon ' ittle ones, the oldest being 74 years, so you know r Hippolyte, who had the care of a house in course of have plenty to do. My little boy Jim, aged 21 years, erection in the Rue Charenton, Paris, has met with a curious misfortune. Being very cold he wrapped him_ was brought up on Vi-Cocoa, And both he and I are the self up in tarpaulin, and lay down near his fire. He fell pictures of health—in fact; all of them are. Sometimes asleep, but was aroused by afeeling of great heat. Some people tell me I should like another kind of cocoa coals had fallen from the grate and burnt his two 1 etter, but I tell them that Vi-Cocoa is at the top of wooden legs to a cinder. The flames spread to his cloth ing, and he could not move. His shouts at last attrac the tree, and I think, after over three years' trial, 1 ted attention. When removed to hospital he was in a ought to know." pitiable condition. I</p>
