      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must mall cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible,not later than Thursday. Correspondents are strongly advised to, write briefly. A short letter has a much greater chance of insertion than a long one. We cannot under ordinary circumstances undertake to return the manuscripts which are not used.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>State Church and Some of Its</head>
        <p>The State Church and Some of Its</p>
        <p>Pretensions. Successors of the Apostles.</p>
        <p>Sir,—With your kind permission I would now fulfil the promise made in my letter which you were good enough to publish in the "Examiner" of 22nd Novem- ber. It is well known to all who take an interest in relig- ious matters that the Priests of the Establishment claim to be the successors of the Apostles in the Chris- tian ministry. And from this high position they are accustomed to look down upon the pastors of other sects as inferior men who presume to minister in holy things without lawful authority. The claim thus arrogantly advanced is so absurd and baseless that one might well pass it by with a smile of incredulity. But unfortunately there are multitudes of good people who willingly and reverently endorse it and in some instances almost worship the claimants. It is desirable, therefore, that the claim should be honestly tested by the New Testament—the Christians' only authorised text book. This is my present task. Social position.—Readers of the Gospels and Epistles know that the Apostles were working men in humble circumstances, of blameless reputation, and pfobably unknown beyond the circle of their friends and neigh- bours. The occupations which they followed did not require much book-learning, and it is very likely that their religious knowledge was limited to an acquaint- ance, more or less, with the Hebrew scriptures. And yet, lie who knew the hearts and attainments of all men, passed by the popular Scribes and Pharisees of His day, and chose these obscure workmen as instruments best fitted to carry His Gospel to the ends of the earth. The would-be successors of these men are a totally different class of people altogether. Drawn,for the most part,from fashionable society, they know not and never intend to know, what a life of toil means. I must admit, how- ever, that they are above the humble men of Galilee in learning. For they are well versed in the theologies of schools, know the mythologies of Greece and Rome, and other subjects which strike one as rather out of place in the curriculum of men whose sole duty is to preach Christ. Calling.—Another remarkable difference between the modern and the original Apostles is seen in their re- spective modes of entering the ministry. The latter were drawn to the work by the direct personal voice of Jesus himself. And so full of the power of God was that call that without hesitation or thought of reward they left all their worldly possessions and friends to follow a stranger who, apparently, had nothing to give them but his own love and a distant hope of a place with himself in the kingdom which, as Jews, they i had long been expecting. How different s the calling of their would-be successors! In the case of these gentlemen, it is either a personal or a family matter, involving a long and tedious process. But honour and gain, and not loss, is the end in view. When the candidate for "Holy Orders" has finished his course of training his caning is completed by a Bishop. This state functionary lays his hands npom him andsends him forth as an Apostle, not to heal the sick, but to grant to penitents the absolution and remission of their sins—a function ',which the genuine Apostles neither claimed nor exercised. (Prayer Book: Visitation of the sick.) And of the Bishop himself, he is only a creature of the State having received his authority from the ruling power to whom, on bonded knees, he had to swear allegiance for the honour and emoluments conferred upon him Titles..—Although the Founder of Christianity was the Son of God as well as the Son of Man he bore no worldly distinctions, but was content to be known among his countrymen as Jesus of Nazareth, the Car- penter. And the humility and unworldliness which he thus personally exemplified, he pressed upon his followers in such language as this: Be ye not called Rabbi : for one is your Master, even Christ: and all ye are brethren. And call no man your father upon the earth: for one is your Father, which is in heaven. But he that is greatest among you shall be your servant." (Matt. Mall. 8,9, 11.) This is the.reason why the Apostles always appear in the New Testament under the names given to them by their parents without any appellations whatever. But the modern Apostles being in reality officers of the State, must not only wear livery, but also carry titles to distinguish them from other men. And with a strong disregard for the fitness of things they actually prefix their names with one of the titles of the Almighty! And this daring presumption of mortal men in appro- priating a title of the living God, has the usual degrees of comparison. The rank and file of the Apostles must content themselves with being "Reverend'; but their superior officers, who live in palacesandsitupon thrones, pose as "Right Reverend," and "Most Reverend Father in God"!</p>
        <p>11 Qualifications. Another amazing discrepnacy between the ancient and the modern Apostles lies in "the signs of Apostleship." Readers of the New Testament know that the Lord endowed his Apostles with power over sickness, disease and death, and on the day of Pentecost he supplemented these powers by the gift of tongues whereby they were enabled to deliver their message in all the languages of the world, These were "the signs" by which they were known, wherever they went, as the authorised messengers of Him Who had sent them to preach th gospel of Salvation. Of these "signs," however, their would-be successors are absolutely barren,and the reason assigned for this lack of Apostolic power is that the age of miracles is past. And surely this reason, even if no other existed, is sufficient to destroy the figment of Apostolic succession. .</p>
        <p>'Means of Support.—ln sending out His Apostles, the Lord did not assign to them parishes and dioceses • the wide world was their mission-field. Neither did he ordain that they should be supported by tithes, pew- rents, or any other charges levied on the people. He Himself had lived and laboured without fee or reward; and surely it was not only a duty but an honour to the Apostles to follow their beloved Master in this respect. Freely they had received the glorious message committed to them, and they must deliverit to a perishing world without money and without price. And how loyally and faithfully they did so may be inferred from the pathetic language of Paul the aged to the elders of Ephesus. These were his words :—"Yea, ye yourselves know, that these hands have ministered unto ray necessities and to them that are with me. I have showed you all things, how that so labouring ye ought to support the weak, and to remember the words of the Lord Jesus, how he said, it is more blessed to give than to receive." (Acts xx. 3.1, 35). And in this connection I may state that "Even as late as A.D. 398, the fourth Council of Carthage directs that Clergymen, however learned they may be in the divine word, should provide themselves with food and clothing by some handicraft or twricul- tarsi labour." (Backhouse and Taylor's Early Church History). But all this is entirely'changed. The modern Apostles are too aristocratic and refined to haul the fisherman's net, handle the tent pole, or guide the agri- cultural piongh. Secular employment of any kind would ill befit their dignity, and so a grateful nation re- Ward.s their services by salaries ranging from £15,000 a year downwards! With all these things before their eyes one need not wonder that the great majority of the people of this country eschew the "Reverend Apostles" altogether. They know that the power which le&amp; the humble men of Galilee to sacrifice all things, even life itself, was love for their beloved Master and their fellow creatures. Why then should they believe in men who go into the ministry, not to suffer loss and persecution, but to ob- tain honours and emoluments.—Yours truly, lONA. Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Tri 'EX A Nil NvR FRI ' Y VC B PLR 12. 1902</head>
        <p>Tri 'EX A Nil NvR FRI ' Y VC B PLR 12. 1902</p>
        <p>we have made enquiries and have received the assurance that every man who went on theplatform with Runcorn is a bona-fide member of that choir, and that the officials are prepared to show to Mr. Payton,or any one else who likes to go over to investigate,their books, and to prove that fact up to the hilt. With regard to Makerfield we have made no en- quiries, as they carried off no prize and came low down (on the same peg as Warrington) and surely the com- mittee cannot be charged with unfairly putting them there. In this connection I might point out the possi- bility (which I believe to be the actual fact in this case) of an individual being a member of two choirs in which case I know of nothing that should prevent him competing with either or both choirs of which he claims membership. Now with regard to Mr. Berrey conducting Runcorn. Mr. Payton's knowledge of Eisteddfods is small indeed or he would never have raised this point. Irrespective of Mr. Berrey being the paid professional vocal trainer of Runcorn, it was quite open to him to conduct every choir in the competition no matter what name was on the programme if he hail been engaged to do so and in this respect Choral Com- petitions do not stand alone. Has Mr. Payton never beard of band contests where one noted conductor has eonducted Most of the bands?</p>
        <p>Now for Mr. Payton's point that Mr. Berrey is a member of the Apollo Choir (whose members were specially disqualified from taking part in the Male Choir competition). A member of the Apollo Choir is a per- son who pays a certain subscription. Mr. Berrey on the contrary is a person who has to be paid a fee for what- ever tuition he gives to us. He is our paid professional vocal trainer. Mr. Thomas Brongh is our conductor. Mr. Berrey only when he is specially engaged. If after this there is any doubt remaining in Mr. Payton's mind that Mr. Berrey is not a member of the Apollo Choir, r would suggest that he consults those members of his own choir who approached Mr. Berrey, while he was training our choir and induced him for a few weeks to take over the training of the Warrington Male Voice Choir, as-to 'whether they thoughtthey were approaching a member of the 'Apollo Choir. I have dealt fully with the points raised by Mr. Payton and I think, whether, or not he is satisfied, that he was beaten by 'fair fighting." I think I have made it clear that the Warrington 'gale Voice Choir entered the competition not "just sinagly to swell the entrance fees to make the concern pay" but with a fair and square chance of the prize and that as far as this committee is concerned he had fair play in a free and open competition. lam sorry to take up so much of your valuable space but must plead the seriousness of this attack on our good name as our excuse. Thanking you in anticipation , I remain yours truly, for the committee of the Apollo Prize Choir, ROBERT W. COOK (hon secretary). 31, Selby-street, Warrington.</p>
