      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>OUR ALMANACK ILLUSTRATION. The Municipal Technical School.</head>
        <p>OUR ALMANACK ILLUSTRATION. The Municipal Technical School.</p>
        <p>The sheet almanack which we present to our readers to-day, contains, Pq will he gem, a photographic view of the opening ceremony of the Municipal Technical School. In the foreground stands the Earl of Derby accompanied by the Mayor and Mayoress, the Mayor of Wigan and a number of prominent townsmen who take an interest in educational matters. For many years it has been thedesire of the Corporation to provides suitable home for the various classes of technical instruction which have been conducted hitherto in several parts of the town. An endeavour was made to raisesufFicient money by voluntary contributions to erect the buildings, but the response to the appeal was disappointing and the Museum Committee had to fall back upon the rates for the money required. • The handsome 'building which has been erected will provide accommodation for some 500 students. The general design of the building is in Later English Renaissance, the main weight of the treatment being kept to the central doorway which it has been thought desirable to emphasize. In order to heighten the effect of the plain brick front, carved brick panels have been introduced, on which have been cut the names of notable scientists and philosophers. The in- terior arrangements are admirably adapted for the different uses and purposes to which they will be put. Accommodation is provided for evening classes in the following departments :—Engineering (including elec- tricity, building and plumbing); chemistry (including metallurgy., iron and steel, and cotton spinning); bio- logy including botany and hygiene) and domestic, science (including cookery, dressmaking, SLc). In the day.time, the school is used by -the students attending the Pupil Teachers' Centre. For the control and management of the institute, there are pro- vided an office, committee room, and masters and mistress's gooms and stores. It is intended at an early 'date to submit to the Town Council plans of further buildings proposed to be erected at the rear of the present buildings. In this further scheme accom- modation will be provided for a Continuation School and Pupil Teachers' Centre' and also a lecture room, which can be used by the different classes, and which can also be used in connection with the Parr Hall or by the public, through a proposed connection with Parr Hall without disturbing the students. When these additions have been made Warrington will be in posses- sion of Technical School Buildings replete inevery, way, and it is to be hoped that the youth of the town will take advantage of the facilities offered for the acquire- ment of technical and useful knowledge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ANOTHER " HAROLD " DISASTER. Alarming Accident in the Ship Canal. Lock Gates Carried Away and Tugboat</head>
        <p>ANOTHER " HAROLD " DISASTER. Alarming Accident in the Ship Canal. Lock Gates Carried Away and Tugboat</p>
        <p>Sunk.</p>
        <p>An accident occured at Barton Lock on Wednesday which recalls the memorable .'Harold" disaster at Latchford a few years ago. The circumstances are pre- cisely the same. After discharging cotton at Manches- ter, the steamship Winkfield went through Barton Lock at a speed sufficient to completely carry away the lower gates. These are now lying at the bottom of the Canal, With them is the tug Flying Breeze, which was collided with by the Winkfield and sunk in the lock. All the men on the tug were, fortunately, able to jump ashore un- injured before their vessel went down. The upper gates were closed with the rush of water and, although damaged, they maintained their position. The inter- mediate gates. which were undamaged, were afterwards closed, and made it possible to open the upper gates and begin to repair them for temporary use until the plant can be brought to replace them by the spare steel gates which are in readiness. The lower gates will be fished out of the lock very soon, and the intermediate gates will be transferred to the lower end otthe lock, which will then have the whole length of 600 feet againavailable. The sunken tug will also be removed with- out delay. The other lock, 350 feet long, is, of course, available for traffic, and it is expected that the large lock, GOO feet long, as already stated, will be again ready for use within three days' time. The Winkfield has suffered practically no damage. She proceeded to Runcorn to discharge her remaining cargb of 1,600 tons of phosphate rock. She is 2,575 tons net register, owned by Messrs. Siveright, Bacon, and Co., and came to Man- chester from Savannah on the sth inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>PEN KETH.</head>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MRS. PlCKTON.—General consternation was felt throughont the village on the sad news of the death of Mrs. Pickton, wife of Mr. William Pickton, the Square, Penketh. The deceased lady was sitting reading her Bible on Sunday night, when her husband, who was in the back kitchen heard a fall, and rushing in found her on the floor. Help was a once secured but life was extinct. The deceased lady was a thorough Christian, and her kind and gentle demeanour to all had made her greatly respected. She was a regular atten- der at the Wesleyan Chapel. The greatest sympathy is felt for the husband of the deceased lady, who was in her 71st year. The interment took place in St. Mary's Cemetery on Wednesday afternoon, in the pre- sence of a large number of relatives and friends. The Rev. J. R. Jones, M.A., vicar, conducted the service, and the Rev. John Gunnell, Wesleyan minister was also present. LIBERAL CUM.—The annual Christmas handicap was concluded last Tuesday evening when H. Davies and D. Matthews met in the final, Davies being the ultimate winner. Good play throughout has been shown, and the excellence of the handicapping, has been proved by some extremely tight finishes. The 3rd and 4th prizes go to J. Bowyer, and F. Dale respectively.</p>
        <p>Serious Cycling Accident in Marsh House-lane. A serious cycling accident occurred in Marsh-house- lane on Monday evening. It appears that Mr. Corbett, I 1135, Padgate-lane, a traveller for Messrs. Richardson and Son, grocers, Bridge-street, was cycling home along Marsh-house-lane about five o'clock. At the . corner of Marsh-street the crown of the front forks of Mr. Corbett's machine snapped completely off, with. the result that the wheel collapsed and Mr. Corbett , was pitched over the handle bars with considerable P force. He was very. severely injured about the head and was picked up m an unconscious condition by a ; number of passers-by, who carried him to the shop of Mr. A. Taylor, fruiterer, Padgate-lane, which is nest door to his own home. Mr. Corbett's wife was away from home at the time, and the injured cyclist was received by Mr. and Mrs. Taylor,who attended to him.l He was put to bed,and Dr. Bennett was sent for. Mr. Corbett having regained consciousness, the doctor placed four stitches in a severe wound over his left eye. His head was also badly injured.</p>
