      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>SOLDIERS AND CITIZENS.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS AND CITIZENS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON INHABITANTS THANKED.</p>
        <p>Presentation to the Mayor.</p>
        <p>There was an interesting ceremony at the Town Hall, Warrington, on Saturday, when Colonel Fitzherbert and other officers of the line, Militia, and Volnnteers attended for the purpose of presenting an illuminated expression of thanks to his Worship, the Corporation and the inhabitants of Warrington, for the care and assistance they have given to the wives and children and other dependents of the men who have served their country in South Africa. The address was artistically illuminated and enclosed in a frame. It read as follows: —"The King's Liverpool Regiment, the Prince of I Wales's Volunteers, South Lancashire Regiment. On the conclusion of the South African campaign, and the consequent return of the troops from the seat of war, I, as commanding officer of the Bth and 40th Regimental Districts, am requested by the troops, Line, Militia and Volunteers, in the command to convey this mark of their sincere appreciation and gratitude to the Mayors of Warrington during the years 1899, 1900, 1901, 1902,t0 the Town Clerk and other officials of the Corporhtion, and particularly to the Borough Treasurer and his staff (as members of the Transvaal Relief Committee of Warrington) for their untiring efforts to relieve dis- tress throughout the war, and for the tact and sympathy they have shown in administering the aid offered to the -wives and families of the men of Warrington serving at the front. lam desired also by the troops to place on record their deep sense of gratitude to the subscribers to the fund so ably administered by the above com- mittee, and to thank all whose care for the wives and families of the married soldiers enabled the latter to meet with stouter hearts and higher courage, the enemy in the field. These kindly actions will not fail to bind still closer the existing ties between his most Gracious Majesty's forces and their fellow countrymen, and in the future should need arise, it will encourage the former to greater efforts, and nerve their arms and steel their hearts to higher sacrifices for their hearths, their coun- try, and their Sovereign.—Signed on behalf of the subscribers to this resolution of thanks.—E. - H. FITZHERBERT, Colonel Commanding 8-40 R.D. Orford Barracks, Warrington, 6th December, 1902." The proceedings took ploce in the Council Chamber and were attended by a representative gathering of the military and civil authorities in Warrington. The Mayor presided and was accompanied •by the Deputy Mayor (Alderman Fairclough) The officers, present were : Orford Barracks: Col. Fitzherbert, Major Hartley, Cap- tains Vaughan and Skinner, Lieutenants, Woods, Bailey, Hutchinson, and Roberts; Volunteers: Col. Ridgway, Major Roome (Adjutant), Col. Buckton, Major flutchin- sdn; Captains Appleton, J. D. Fairclough,B. Fairclough, G. R. Crosileld, C. L. Williamson, and Haslam ; Lieuten- ants E. Ridgway and Fulton Smith, and Sergeant-Major Rawlinson and 19 other Volunteers. The civilians pre- sent included Alderman Smethurst; Councillors William Roberts, Hulse, Bennett, Baird, and Brophy ; Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), Mr J Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr. Ker (deputy borough surveyor), Dr. Gornall (medical officer of health), the Mayoress and the Misses Parr, Mr. P. Monks, Mr. H. Roberts, Mr. R. Byerley, and Mr. Joseph R. Williams.</p>
        <p>SPEECH BY COLONEL FITZEIERBERT. SOLDIERS" NOT IDLE GILDED BUTTERFLIES." Immediately on the Mayor taking his seat, Colonel FITZHERBERT said: I come here on behalf of all branches of the Imperial forces to return thanks for the kind contributions of your town and the aid given to the wives and families of the soldiers serving in South Africa. Believe me we are not ungrateful, and we fully appreciate the kindness and the generosity which have enabled these families to live in ease and comfort while the bread-winner was in South Africa. Charity is no charity, in ray opinion, if it is badly administered, but lam able from my position on the Relief Committee to say that the fund has been adminis- tered with tact, judgment, and generosity. We feel that we owe a very great debt indeed to the Borough Treasurer and his subordinates for the manner in Which they have acted. I don't believe there is a single town in England where the work has been done better. Furthermore we beg to recognize the noble self-sacrifice of the working men of this town in the weekly con- tributions they have given to the fund, without which it must have inevitably collapsed. We are all Fnglish- men, whatever our politics and whichever way we think, and there is not one who is not always ready to do his share of work that the Union Jack may fly free and maintain the supremacy of the Empire. (Applause.) I think that on this occasion, when I am about to quit your town, having only been here a compara- tively short time, I may say that experience has shown me that Lancashire is Lancashire all through. (Hear, hear, and applause.) When I was in -my old depot at Lancaster I ne,ver appealed to the town for assistance to the soldier with- out receiving a ready response. I came here unknown and a stranger, but the Soldiers' Club in War-, rington stands as a record of the generosity of the town. Waf is a fearful and an awful thing, but there is a silver lining to every cloud and if the citizens of the country have learnt to sympathize with their soldiers, if they have learnt that this great Empire cannot be attained and maintained without blood and sacrifice, and that those who are not engaged in the war have a duty devolving upon them namely to alleviate suffering at home, they have learnt a lesson of some value. (Applause.) The time has now come for me to retire from my position as officer commanding the depot at Warrington, and in doing so I wish to give von, Mr. thanks Mayor, my most sincere :auks for the kinaness and courtesy you and your Council have ever shown to me. I think the war has shown one thing, and has done one good thing—it has drawn together the ci,:zen and the soldier. (Hear, hear.) The citizen has learnt to know that we are not the idle gilded butterflies wearepainted; that we are, in fact, fellow citizens, dressed in scarlet, wearing the livery of our King, striving hard to do our duty and to obey the commands of our Sovereign. (Applause.) To us as officers, the lives of our soldiers are very dear to us, and the comfort of their wives and families is to us of infinite moment. (Applause.) I now ask the Brigade-Major to read the address express- ing our feelings. Major Roome then read the address. THE MAYOR'S REPLY. The MAYOR, in accepting the address, said: On behalf of our county borough and its municipal Council. .on behalf of my predecessors in office, and last, and T think I ought to say least, on my own behalf—for I feel that mine has been an infinitesimal proportion in the good work for which we are receiving your kind acknowledg- ment—l say on behalf of all concerned I thank you most heartily and sincerely for this token of your recog- nition and appreciation of our services. It was not only a pleasant duty, but a privilege to be able to assist from our civic point of view in the great military enter. prise which our Empire felt called upon to undertake, an enterprise which, through the indomitable energy and determination of our Government, the skill and courage of our officers, and the pluck and heroism of our soldiers and sailors, we have been able after a protracted and costly struggle to bring to a successful and triumphant issue. (Apr' use.) It was</p>
