        <p>The inquiry into the circumstances surrounding the i death of the boy IVlitton Simpson, as detailed in our last issue was held at the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Friday, by Mr S Brighouse coroner. Mr Bland from the solicitor's office of the London and North-Western Railway Company was present, and prior to the open- I ing of the evidence produced plans showing the spot where the accident happened. Frances Fowles age 12, of 18, Derby-road, Vulcan, stated that on Tuesday last some t me between 12.30 and one o'clock, she was going home in the direction of Vulcan when she saw five young children playing near the crossing. She saw that they were going to runacross the lines. She saw the train coming and succeeded in pulling three of the children back, but one got across hd deceased stumbled and was run over. —ln answer te ne Coroner, witness said they had just got through the wicket gate when she saw them. They were going to run across the lines when she got hold of three of them. Deceased had nearly got over the second metal when the train came. Witness said that deceased was quite half a mile away from home, and there was nothing to protect the children from playing on the line. The wicket gate was opened. The high banks on either sideg of the metals prevented anyone seeing a train comm. round the curve. A Juror: This particular place is very dangerous It is not long ago since Miss Bridge was killed at the same spot. It is a death trap to children. The Coroner: If you examine all the history of a level crossing you will find that something had happened at nearly all crossings in 20 or 30 years. Mather Ives, an engine driver on the London and North-Western Railway, deposed to being on the train from Bolton to Warrington, which arrives at Bridge's crossing at 12.50. When about four or five hundred yards away from the crossing he opened his whistle. When he was almost 20 yards away from the crossing ; he saw the deceased rim across the line. He immediately applied the brakes but found that the child had been caught by the front guard. When picked up deceased was quite dead. The Coroner said that there was no one to blame for the regrettable accident. When the railway was bull tit was in accordance with the regulations of the Board of Trade. In those days the public were not so much alive to the dangers of level crossings as they are now. It was only quite recently that .the public had been ablete get across the level crossings in safety. It was the duty of the jurymen to recommend what was reasonable for the company to do to guard the public safety. Whatever the jury might recommend the presentment would be sent not only to the company, but to the Board of Trade. A Juror asked whether the Urban District Council had anything to do with the crossing. The Coroner said he would also send a copy of the verdict, and the jury's presentment to the clerk of the Newton Urban District Council. A Juror: Is it the moral duty of the Railway Com- pany or the District Council to see to the crossings? NORTHERN RUGBY UNION. The Coroner: I should simply suggest what you might reaaonably expect the company to do. THE COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP. • , The jury retired, and after a few minutes' deliberation brought in a verdict of "Accidental death," and added -- a presentment that a footbridge should be constructed' LANCASHIRE V. NORTHUMBERLAND AND over the lines, and that the Urban District Council of DURHAM. Newton put themselves in communication with the company to further the object; and further, that either I This rather unimportant County game was played at 1 I Broughton before 2,000 people. Fish and Lomas were sides of the banks near the crossing be reduced in size. absentees for the Lancashire side, Harris and Morgan A Juror asked whether the company were prepared to ; filling the vacancies. Tqe teams turned out to time, and early on Dune- render any financial assistance to the father of the ' deceased, as he was very poor andhad a wife and family 1 shire asserted their superiority. Morgan mulled the to keep. Mr. Bland said he would bring that before the COM-home team attacked strongly. return from the visitors' kick off, but straightway the pany, and he was sure that they would do something in' Wilson went to the lines of the combined counties, but Thompson managed to that way. The Foreman of the jury said that it was their inten- relieve. A minute later Evans crossed for Lancashire's tion to get up a subscription for the benefit of the first try, Ferguson missing the goal. The game was no sooner restarted than the Lancashire backs combined father, splendidly, and Wallwork passing to Wilson he ran over, I for S. James to land the goal. These points seemed to rouse the visitors, and a neat effort ended in Gordon scoring. Parker missed theattempt at goal. From here to half-time Lancashire did most of the pressing, and Hogg should have crossed from a pass by Wilson, the former not taking the ball. Then Harris seemed a cer- tain scorer, but Sinclair managed to tackle him. Near- ing the interval Lancashire were dangerons,but, throwing away several fine chances, had not augmented their score up to half-time. For some time in the second half the play was even, but it was apparent that Lancashire were easily holding the upper hand. Once or twice the visitors looked likely to score, but they were weak near the line, and good opportunities were thrown away. A case of infringe- ment of the rules by Northumberland gave Lancashire is penalty kick in a favourable position. and S. James re- gistered a goal. After this the visitors had a fair share of the play. Nearing the Lancashire lines Guy dropped , a goal, and the final score was :—Lancashire, two goals I and two tries (ten points); Northumberland and Dur- ham, one goal and one try (five points). The following were the teams:— Lancashire: D. Smith (Salford), back; A. Hogg and R. Wilson (Broughton Rangers),W.Wallwork (Swinton), and E. Harris (Warrington), threequarters ; S. James (Broughton Rangers) and E. Morgan (Swinton), half backs; J. Rhapps, P. Tunney, and G. Heath (Salford), . G. Frater and J Ferguson (Oldham), J. Evans (Swinton, ; W. Orem (Broughton Rangers), and G. Boardman (Leigh, forwards. I Northumberland and Durham: H. Sinclair (Hull ! Kingston Rovers), back ; J. Deakin (South Shields), J. ; E. Parker (Bramley), J. Gordon (Hull Kingston Rovers). and W. Hector (South Shields), threequarter backs; W. Guy (Hull Kingson Rovers) and J. Thompson (Hull), half backs; J. Ritson (Hull) It. Shaw (Salford), E. E. Rawlings (Barrow), C. W. CA, R. Thompson, J. Jewitt, , R. Hindson and J. Smith (South Shields), forwards. ' Referee, J. Bruckshaw, Cheshire; touch judges, J. H. Houghton (Lancashire) and J. Morreli (Northumber- land and Durham). d THE LEAGUE.---FIRST DIVISION. -- HUDDERSFIELD v,. WIDNES. There was only a small attendance to witness this match at Faxtown. Half-time arrived with the score nil. After the intertal Widnes attacked strongly. The visitors swarmed on the home lines and Lally dropped&amp; goal. A forward movement by Huddersfield relieved, and then Holroyd was noticeable with a tricky rim. Kitchen then misfielded the ball, and Hughes dribbled over the line, but Larard kicked deadwhen a try looked certain. The game had now got very keen, and twice Peacock was almost over, but Kitchen tackled. spien- didly. The ball was soon after kicked to Kitchen, who feebly took a flying kick, and let the visitors in, and Power scored a try, which Tilley converted. Hudders- fl7f d then attacked, and first Larard and then Deere made desperate efforts to score. At this stage Kitchen had to retire, his leg giving way-. At midway Sutcliffe chargedTilley's kick, and gathering the ball scored a ' Splendid try, and Holroyd kicked a goal. In the next minute Taylor dropped a goal for Widnes. This reverse stirred up the home team, who were attacking strongly on the call of time. Result :—Widnes, 2 goals and 1 try (7 points), Huddersfield 1 goal and I try (5 points). Teams : Huddersfield.—W. F. Kitchen, back; L. I. Deere, W. Dewhurst, Larard, and J. F. A. Windle, three-quarter backs; P. Holroyd and F. Dewhurst, half backs; E. V. Chambers. P. Driver, E. Dawson, E. Knight, F. Little- wood, A. Barraclough, S. Pool, and M. Sutcliffe, for- wards. Widnes.—Tilley, back; Peacock, Taylor, Rispan, and Hughes, three-quarter backs; Douglas and Lally, half- backs; Aspey, Curtis, Turner, Leyshon, Rigby, Riley, , Power, and Webster, forwards. . I Referee, Mr. B. Ashton, Oldham. Touch judges , Messrs. A. Brook, Brighouse, and H.Farrer,Manningham' SALFORD v. HULL. This fixture took place at the Willows, Weaste, before 6,000 people. At half-time there was no score. On re- suming,Salford were soon attacking, and were credited with a rather lucky try. Jacques had his kick charged down, and Bell getting possession ran over at the corner. Brown failed at goal. Cook was ever prominent for the visitors, and led no to the try which equalised matters, the scorer being Parry. In the last few minutes Hull almost won, a drop by Cook just failing, the ball passing under the bar. Result—Salford 1 try (3 points), Hull 1 try (3 points).</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON RAILWAY SERVANT CHARGED WITH ROBBERY. At the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday before Alderman Sutton and other magistrates, Thomas O'Brien, a carter in the employ of the LondonandNorth Western Railway Company, was charged with stealing three skin rugs, of the value of £3 from, the goods warehouse at ' Bank Quay.—Mr. E. A. Connor represented the prisoner. —Detective Howells stated that on Tuesday afternoon he was in the goods warehouse when a complaint was recpived from Messrs. Pendlebury and Co., Winwick- street, that three skin rugs were missing from a parcel. On making inquiries he found that the rugs had been in the possession of the prisoner and two of them had been sold. He asked for a remand for a week.—Mr. Connor, who defended,asked that thecase should be beard forth,- with, and said that he would advise his client to plead; guilty if that could be done.--Detective Howells said there had been several robberies reported to the com- pany, and •18 prisoner was only arrested at nine o'clock on Tuesday night there had been no time to consult the company's solicitor.—The remand was granted. we. COMMERCIAL FAILURES.—According to "Kernps Mercantile Gazette," the number of New Bankruptcy Proceedings in England and Wales gazetted during the week ending December 6th, was 93. The number in the corresponding week of last year was 89. showing an increase of 4, being a net decrease in 1902, to date, of 9.</p>
